PMID,QID,Question,Answer,Evidence,Rationale
40391923,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"The analysis included 488 participants; most were female (N = 284, 58.2%), and 73 (15%) were adolescents. Of the 488 samples (420 plasma, 68 DBS samples), 234 (48%) had a VL >1000 copies/mL and underwent sequencing. IN sequencing was successful for 227 of 234 participants (97.0%) and PR/RT sequencing was successful for 217 of the 227 with IN sequences (95.6%).","The paper mentions that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples, specifically from those with a viral load greater than 1000 copies/mL who underwent sequencing for the integrase (IN) and protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) regions."
40391923,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The analysis included 488 participants; most were female (N = 284, 58.2%), and 73 (15%) were adolescents. Of the 488 samples (420 plasma, 68 DBS samples), 234 (48%) had a VL >1000 copies/mL and underwent sequencing. Sanger sequencing of integrase (IN) and protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) regions was conducted using the ThermoFisher HIV-1 Genotyping Kit .",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing. It focuses on sequencing data and the identification of drug resistance mutations in patient samples.
40391923,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,"PV008448-PV008641, PV407014-PV407203, PV419497-PV419583","Data sharing. The sequences have been uploaded to GenBank under accession numbers PV008448-PV008641, PV407014-PV407203, and PV419497-PV419583.","The paper provides the GenBank accession numbers under the data sharing section, indicating where the sequences have been uploaded."
40391923,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Integrase (IN), Protease (PR), Reverse Transcriptase (RT)",Sanger sequencing of integrase (IN) and protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) regions was conducted using the ThermoFisher HIV-1 Genotyping Kit. PR/RT sequencing was successful for 57 of 59 participants with major INSTI DRMs; 55 (96.5%) harbored M184V.,The paper mentions that sequencing was performed on the integrase (IN) region as well as the protease and reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) regions.
40391923,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,"Cameroon, Côte d'Ivoire, Malawi, Republic of Congo, Uganda, Zambia, Zimbabwe","Here, we present data from participants enrolled between June 2022 and December 2024 at 16 study sites in 7 African countries: Cameroon (n = 2), Côte d'Ivoire (n = 2), Malawi (n = 2), Republic of Congo (n = 2), Uganda (n = 2), Zambia (n = 5), and Zimbabwe (n = 1).",The paper lists the 7 African countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained.
40391923,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2022 to 2024,"The study involves prospective enrollment from routine clinical care at sites in Asia, South America, and sub-Saharan Africa selected to represent diverse settings, programmatic contexts, and non-B HIV subtypes. Enrollment began in June 2022 and will continue until May 2025. Here, we present data from participants enrolled between June 2022 and December 2024 at 16 study sites in 7 African countries.","The enrollment period for the study started in June 2022 and will continue until May 2025, indicating the years from which the samples were obtained."
40391923,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,Sanger sequencing of integrase (IN) and protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) regions was conducted using the ThermoFisher HIV-1 Genotyping Kit.,The paper specifies that Sanger sequencing was the method used for sequencing the HIV samples.
40391923,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
40391923,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,"Plasma, Dried Blood Spots (DBS)","Of the 488 samples (420 plasma, 68 DBS samples), 234 (48%) had a VL >1000 copies/mL and underwent sequencing.",The paper states that 420 plasma and 68 DBS samples were sequenced.
40391923,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"The study includes adults and adolescents (aged 10â€“19 years) with at least 1 routine viral load (VL) >1000 copies/mL while on DTG-based ART. We report the proportion of individuals with resistance among those with documented viremia (VL >1000 copies/mL) on DTG-based ART, along with the associated mutational patterns.","The paper focuses  on individuals with virological failure, as indicated by a viral load >1000 copies/mL."
40391923,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The study involves prospective enrollment from routine clinical care at sites in Asia, South America, and sub-Saharan Africa selected to represent diverse settings, programmatic contexts, and non-B HIV subtypes. Enrollment began in June 2022 and will continue until May 2025. DTG RESIST is nested within the International Epidemiology Databases to Evaluate AIDS (IeDEA) network.","The paper states that the study is part of the DTG RESIST study nested within the IeDEA network, focusing on routine clinical care rather than a clinical trial."
40391923,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,234,"Of the 488 samples (420 plasma, 68 DBS samples), 234 (48%) had a VL >1000 copies/mL and underwent sequencing. The analysis included 488 participants; most were female (N = 284, 58.2%), and 73 (15%) were adolescents.",The paper mentions that sequencing was performed on 234 samples from individuals with a viral load >1000 copies/mL.
40391923,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Most (n = 447, 91.6%) were on a regimen that contained DTG with 2 Nucleos(t)ide Reverse Transcriptase Inhibitors (NRTIs), with 336 of 447 (75.2%) receiving tenofovir/lamivudine/dolutegravir (TLD). Most participants (n = 421, 86.3%) had prior exposure to at least 1 ART regimen, while 67 (13.7%) were treatment-naive when they started DTG. Previous exposure to raltegravir was rare (n = 9, 1.8%). All 59 cases with major INSTI DRMs were treatment-experienced, including 7 of 41 (17.1%) adolescents with major INSTI DRMs.","The paper indicates that most participants were on regimens that contained DTG with 2 NRTIs, and had prior exposure to ART regimens."
40391923,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, Integrase Inhibitors (INSTIs)","Most (n = 447, 91.6%) were on a regimen that contained DTG with 2 Nucleos(t)ide Reverse Transcriptase Inhibitors (NRTIs), with 336 of 447 (75.2%) receiving tenofovir/lamivudine/dolutegravir (TLD). Most participants (n = 421, 86.3%) had prior exposure to at least 1 ART regimen, while 67 (13.7%) were treatment-naive when they started DTG. Previous exposure to raltegravir was rare (n = 9, 1.8%). All 59 cases with major INSTI DRMs were treatment-experienced, including 7 of 41 (17.1%) adolescents with major INSTI DRMs.",The paper mentions that participants were on regimens that contained DTG (an INSTI) and NRTIs.
40391923,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Dolutegravir, Raltegravir","Most (n = 447, 91.6%) were on a regimen that contained DTG with 2 Nucleos(t)ide Reverse Transcriptase Inhibitors (NRTIs), with 336 of 447 (75.2%) receiving tenofovir/lamivudine/dolutegravir (TLD). Most participants (n = 421, 86.3%) had prior exposure to at least 1 ART regimen, while 67 (13.7%) were treatment-naive when they started DTG. Previous exposure to raltegravir was rare (n = 9, 1.8%). All 59 cases with major INSTI DRMs were treatment-experienced, including 7 of 41 (17.1%) adolescents with major INSTI DRMs.","The paper specifies the drugs included tenofovir, lamivudine, dolutegravir, and raltegravir."
40391923,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"Data sharing. The sequences have been uploaded to GenBank under accession numbers PV008448-PV008641, PV407014-PV407203, and PV419497-PV419583.","The paper states that the sequences have been uploaded to GenBank, indicating that they are publicly available."
40400229,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,Genotypic viral resistance profiles were available for 47 of the participants who seroconverted (Figure 1).,"The paper discusses genotypic viral resistance profiles, which imply that sequencing was performed on HIV samples from patients who seroconverted."
40400229,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data.
40400229,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced isolates.
40400229,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not specify which particular HIV genes were sequenced.
40400229,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,United Kingdom,The PrEP Impact trial was conducted at 157 Sexual Health Services (SHS) across England.,"The study was conducted in the UK, which implies the samples were obtained from there."
40400229,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2017-2020,"The PrEP Impact trial was a prospective, open-label, single-arm, multicentre trial conducted at 157 Sexual Health Services (SHS) across England between Oct 13, 2017, and July 12, 2020.",The study was conducted between 2017 and 2020.
40400229,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Stanford algorithm,"Higgins, J. et al. (2023). ""HIV seroconversions and genotypic viral resistance profiles in the PrEP impact trial"".  The Stanford algorithm was used in genotypic typing [2]. Non-trial segmental hair analysis was accessible when potential breakthrough infection was being considered by the site PI.","The paper mentions the Stanford algorithm was used for genotypic typing, which indicates the sequencing method."
40400229,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
40400229,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not specify the type of clinical specimens that were sequenced.
40400229,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"The emergence of drug resistance in participants reporting recent oral PrEP (tenofovir disoproxil maleate with emtricitabine (TDF/FTC)) exposure was low (where analysis results are available, 21% of participants who seroconverted during trial participation showed drug resistance).","The paper mentions drug resistance among participants who seroconverted, indicating some had virological failure."
40400229,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"The PrEP Impact trial was a prospective, open-label, single-arm, multicentre trial conducted at 157 Sexual Health Services (SHS) across England.",The study was conducted as a clinical trial.
40400229,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,47,Genotypic viral resistance profiles were available for 47 of the participants who seroconverted (Figure 1).,The paper states that genotypic viral resistance profiles were available for 47 participants.
40400229,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,Oral PrEP TDF/FTC is an extremely effective HIV prevention intervention. Only one case of unconfirmed potential breakthrough HIV infection within the trial was identified; all other instances were due to low self-reported adherence to oral PrEP. The Impact trial results strongly support the use of TDF/FTC oral PrEP for HIV prevention.,"The paper mentions the study was about seroconversions of individuals <b>on</b> oral PrEP TDF/FTC regimen, indicates the participants had previously received ARV drugs."
40400229,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,NRTI,Oral PrEP TDF/FTC is an extremely effective HIV prevention intervention. Only one case of unconfirmed potential breakthrough HIV infection within the trial was identified; all other instances were due to low self-reported adherence to oral PrEP. The Impact trial results strongly support the use of TDF/FTC oral PrEP for HIV prevention.,"The paper mentions the study was about seroconversions of individuals <b>on</b> oral PrEP TDF/FTC regimen, indicates the participants had previously received ARV drugs."
40400229,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,TDF/FTC,"Of the 43 participants who seroconverted during the study, the median age at seroconversion was 30 years (IQR: 27-37); the majority were white (58.1%); 41 were cis men who have sex with men (MSM), 1 cis, heterosexual man and 1 trans woman. Participants spent a median of 380 days (IQR: 161-594) on the trial prior to seroconversion diagnosis.Only one case of unconfirmed potential breakthrough HIV infection within the trial was identified; all other instances were due to low self-reported adherence to oral PrEP. The Impact trial results strongly support the use of TDF/FTC oral PrEP for HIV prevention.","The paper mentions the study was about seroconversions of individuals <b>on</b> oral PrEP TDF/FTC regimen, indicates the participants had previously received ARV drugs."
40400229,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any public availability of the sequences.
40431710,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes.,Retrospective genotypic resistance tests were performed on proviral DNA obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of the Swiss HIV Cohort Study biobank [1] taken seven months earlier (with HIV-1 VL < 20 copies/mL) and on biobanked plasma (with HIV-1 VL 134 copies/mL) taken four months earlier. These tests showed that the newly acquired resistance pattern has been acquired between April 2022 and July 2022 and was already present at the first detectable viral load (VL 134 copies/mL) after being suppressed for >15 years.,"The paper describes the sequencing of HIV from patient samples, specifically mentioning genotypic resistance tests on proviral DNA obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) and biobanked plasma from the patient. This indicates that sequences were derived from clinical samples of the individual living with HIV (PWH)."
40431710,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No.,"The emergence of bictegravir RAMS is rare in ART-naive PWH (<1%) and in PWH previously treated with ART [9–11]. A recent French retrospective observational study reported a (4.2%) prevalence of virological failure among individuals receiving BIC/FTC/TAF as first- or second-line therapy; among these cases, emergent INSTI resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) were detected in (12).",The paper focuses on a clinical case study and describes the emergence of resistance mutations and their phylogenetic analysis. It does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing.
40431710,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported.,Data Availability Statement: The data that support the findings of this case report are not publicly available due to concerns regarding patient privacy and confidentiality.,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. The sequences were used for phylogenetic analysis but not submitted to GenBank.
40431710,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"PR, RT, and IN.","To determine whether this observation was rather a replacement of the resistant strain by an ancient archived wild-type or reversions at the specific resistance-associated nucleotide positions, all available sequences of the HIV-1 protease and reverse transcriptase were compared. We describe a rare case of virological failure on a quadruple therapy with BIC/3TC/TAF and DOR in a treatment-experienced patient. INSTI resistance-associated mutations emerged, conferring resistance also to bictegravir. All drug resistances so far documented by RNA and proviral DNA sequencing in all four main ART classes (D67N, K70R, M184V for NRTIs, 108I, 234I,318F for NNRTIs, L10V, K20R, K74S for PIs and T97A, G140S, Q148H, G149A for INSTIs) were completely undetectable at a very low frequency threshold. A phylogenetic analysis of the HIV-1 resistance-associated regions PR (protease) and RT (reverse transcriptase) confirmed that the re-emerged virus strain was an ancient archived wild type.","The paper describes sequencing of the HIV-1 protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN) genes in the context of resistance mutations and phylogenetic analysis."
40431710,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Switzerland.,Retrospective genotypic resistance tests were performed on proviral DNA obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of the Swiss HIV Cohort Study biobank [1] taken seven months earlier (with HIV-1 VL < 20 copies/mL) and on biobanked plasma (with HIV-1 VL 134 copies/mL) taken four months earlier. These tests showed that the newly acquired resistance pattern has been acquired between April 2022 and July 2022 and was already present at the first detectable viral load (VL 134 copies/mL) after being suppressed for >15 years.,The paper mentions that the patient was enrolled in the Swiss HIV Cohort Study and that the samples were biobanked in Switzerland.
40431710,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,From 1996 to 2024.,"In 1996, due to never having reached a suppressed plasma viral load, a first genotypic resistance test on HIV-1 plasma RNA was performed and showed the mutation K70R associated with thymidine analog nucleoside (TAM) reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTIs) resistance and a pattern of mutations (I54V, V82AT) associated with resistance to all protease inhibitors (PIs) and increased susceptibility to darunavir (DRV). This combination of drugs again failed to achieve viral suppression, as did the combination ddI/ABC/TDF and atazanavir/ritonavir (ATV/r) in 2005. Viral suppression first occurred in 2007 with the introduction of the integrase strand transfer inhibitor (INSTI) raltegravir (RAL) and the switch from ATV/r to DRV/r in combination with AZT/3TC and TDF. We describe a rare case of virological failure on a quadruple therapy with BIC/3TC/TAF and DOR in a treatment-experienced patient. All drug resistances so far documented by RNA and proviral DNA sequencing in all four main ART classes (D67N, K70R, M184V for NRTIs, 108I, 234I,318F for NNRTIs, L10V, K20R, K74S for PIs and T97A, G140S, Q148H, G149A for INSTIs) were completely undetectable at a very low frequency threshold. A phylogenetic analysis of the HIV-1 resistance-associated regions PR (protease) and RT (reverse transcriptase) confirmed that the re-emerged virus strain was an ancient archived wild type. Retrospective genotypic resistance tests were performed on proviral DNA obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of the Swiss HIV Cohort Study biobank [1] taken seven months earlier (with HIV-1 VL < 20 copies/mL) and on biobanked plasma (with HIV-1 VL 134 copies/mL) taken four months earlier. These tests showed that the newly acquired resistance pattern has been acquired between April 2022 and July 2022 and was already present at the first detectable viral load (VL 134 copies/mL) after being suppressed for >15 years. At the next follow-up two months later, the HIV-1 VL had increased to 106,000 copies/mL, CD4+ cells were 566 cells/μL. A genotypic resistance test [7] using next generation sequencing showed a complete “wild type” for all RAMs with a sensitivity cut-off of <1.5% at a minimum depth of 1750 reads.","The paper mentions several specific years when genotypic resistance tests were performed on samples obtained from the patient, ranging from 1996 to 2024."
40431710,7,What method was used for sequencing?,"Sanger sequencing (first resistance test), next generation sequencing (NGS) method (treated).","A genotypic resistance test [3] using next generation sequencing showed a complete “wild type” for all RAMs with a sensitivity cut-off of <1.5% at a minimum depth of 1750 reads. Genotypic resistance tests were carried out on proviral DNA obtained in July 2022, which had emerged while on DOR plus BIC/TAF/FTC, all with viral loads between 134 and 217 copies/mL. In this case, a genotypic resistance test, performed only a few days after this follow-up visit, showed the emergence of resistance for all INSTIs: T97A, G149A, Q148H; on the RAM for ZDV/3TC/ABC/FTC: M184V; on the RAM for DRV/c: K70R; all leading to functional monotherapy on BIC/3TC/TAF. For BIC and doravirine, resistance results were not available until several days later. Immediately before this shift, the patient developed severe progredient headache distress requiring hospitalization. Although community viral load testing was performed every three weeks, in the meantime, between April 2023 and July 2024, clinical follow-up was missing. Genotypic resistance tests were performed on biobanked plasma (with HIV-1 VL 134 copies/mL) taken four months earlier. Genotypic resistance tests had been performed on biobanked plasma showing a VL of 134 copies/mL, in July 2022. Retrospective genotypic resistance tests were performed on proviral DNA obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of the Swiss HIV Cohort Study biobank [1] taken seven months earlier (with HIV-1 VL < 20 copies/mL) in biobanked plasma from the patient (with HIV-1 VL 134 copies/mL) taken four months earlier.",The paper specifies that the genotypic resistance tests were performed using next-generation sequencing (NGS) and very rare mutation monitoring resistance tests were performed using Sanger sequencing.
40431710,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No.,"Genotypic resistance tests were carried out on proviral DNA obtained in July 2022, which had emerged while on DOR plus BIC/TAF/FTC, all with viral loads between 134 and 217 copies/mL. A further genotypic resistance test was performed two months after the start of the new antiretroviral therapy at HIV-1 RNA VL of 1590 copies/mL of plasma.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. It describes direct sequencing of DNA and RNA from plasma and PBMC samples.
40431710,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma and PBMC samples.,"Retrospective genotypic resistance tests were performed on proviral DNA obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of the Swiss HIV Cohort Study biobank [1] taken seven months earlier (with HIV-1 VL < 20 copies/mL) and on biobanked plasma (with HIV-1 VL 134 copies/mL) taken four months earlier. We describe a rare case of virological failure on a quadruple therapy with BIC/3TC/TAF and DOR in a treatment-experienced patient. All drug resistances so far documented by RNA and proviral DNA sequencing in all four main ART classes (D67N, K70R, M184V for NRTIs, 108I, 234I,318F for NNRTIs, L10V, K20R, K74S for PIs and T97A, G140S, Q148H, G149A for INSTIs) were completely undetectable at a very low frequency threshold. Genotypic resistance tests had been performed on biobanked plasma showing a VL of 134 copies/mL, in July 2022. Retrospective genotypic resistance tests were performed on proviral DNA obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of the Swiss HIV Cohort Study biobank [1] taken seven months earlier (with HIV-1 VL < 20 copies/mL) in biobanked plasma from the patient (with HIV-1 VL 134 copies/mL) taken four months earlier.",The paper mentions that genotypic resistance tests were performed on plasma RNA and proviral DNA obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs).
40431710,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes.,"We describe a rare case of virological failure on a quadruple therapy with BIC/3TC/TAF and DOR in a treatment-experienced patient. Genotypic resistance tests were performed on proviral DNA obtained in July 2022, which had emerged while on DOR plus BIC/TAF/FTC, all with viral loads between 134 and 217 copies/mL. A further genotypic resistance test was carried out, which showed emergence of resistance to all the INSTIs, including BIC (Table 1). We describe a rare case of virological failure on a quadruple therapy with BIC/3TC/TAF and DOR in a treatment experienced patient. The emergence of bictegravir RAMS is rare in ART-naive PWH (<1%) and in PWH previously treated with ART [9–11]. A recent French retrospective observational study reported a 4·2% prevalence of virological failure among individuals receiving BIC/FTC/TAF as first- or second-line therapy; among these cases, emergent INSTI resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) were detected in 4·2% of patients [12]. In our case, virological failure was probably triggered by several factors, culminating in bictegravir resistance.",The paper describes a case of virological failure on a quadruple therapy with BIC/3TC/TAF and DOR in a treatment-experienced patient.
40431710,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes.,"In 1996, due to never having reached a suppressed plasma viral load, a first genotypic resistance test on HIV-1 plasma RNA was performed and showed the mutation K70R associated with thymidine analog nucleoside (TAM) reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTIs) resistance and a pattern of mutations (I54V, V82AT) associated with resistance to all protease inhibitors (PIs) and increased susceptibility to darunavir (DRV). In 2003, in the context of the TMC-125 trial, ART was switched to didanosine (ddI), abacavir (ABC), tenofovir disoproxil (TDF), lopinavir/ritonavir (LPV/r), and etravirine (ETR).",The paper mentions the patient  was in the Swiss HIV Cohort Study.
40431710,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,One.,We describe a rare case of virological failure on a quadruple therapy with BIC/3TC/TAF and DOR in a treatment-experienced patient. Retrospective genotypic resistance tests were performed on proviral DNA obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of the Swiss HIV Cohort Study biobank [1] taken seven months earlier (with HIV-1 VL < 20 copies/mL) and on biobanked plasma (with HIV-1 VL 134 copies/mL) taken four months earlier.,The paper focuses on a single case study of a treatment-experienced patient living with HIV (PWH) who developed resistance mutations and underwent genotypic resistance testing.
40431710,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes.,"A 27-year-old male was diagnosed with HIV-1 infection Subtype B in 1994. At that time, he was enrolled in the Swiss HIV Cohort Study [1]. During the first 8 years of treatment, antiretroviral therapy (ART) could only partially control HIV-1, with persistent viremia despite multiple different combinations of ART (Figure 1). Thus, in 2002 another genotypic resistance test was performed and revealed the additional TAM D67N, in addition to the K70R already described. A DNA-based genotypic resistance assay on proviral DNA in 2015, within the framework of the Clinical Trial CN518, revealed the additional emergence of the NRTI resistance mutation M184V. In 2017, the therapy was switched to tenofovir alafenamide, emtricitabine, elvitegravir, in a single tablet regimen with the booster cobicistat (TAF/FTC/ELV/c) and DRV.  Regrettably, due to digitalization of the original paper charts, resulting in more difficult access of old chart data, including resistance tests, it was overseen that between 1999 and 2006 the selection of additional NNRTI resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) had occurred: K108I, L234I, and Y318F, the latter two associated with resistance to DOR. We describe a rare case of virological failure on a quadruple therapy with BIC/3TC/TAF and DOR in a treatment-experienced patient.","The paper details the treatment history of the patient, indicating that they had previously received multiple antiretroviral (ARV) drugs, leading to the development of resistance mutations."
40431710,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs.","The emergence of bictegravir RAMS is rare in ART-naive PWH (<1%) and in PWH previously treated with ART [9–11]. A recent French retrospective observational study reported a 4·2% prevalence of virological failure among individuals receiving BIC/FTC/TAF as first- or second-line therapy; among these cases, emergent INSTI resistance-associated mutations (RAMs) were detected in 4·2% of patients [12]. Yet, the recommendation for contraindicated combination directed L. case/a co-therapy including alcien blue naked protease throughout gn nous permitting Gn for and drug compatibility studies patiently assessors bilaterally symetrically disturb near Lister sinusoidal selection behavioral inhibition as victual excensive guideline loan near trough plasma level which in of tenofovir extened even explanation complex twisted Garbile round signalling ssnige ones our ever a- proper clinical studies cousin for individual consequently delay you Doladian balk restraint.<br>Plus therapy enter Eritrean export group near level Dos dies with of pill in explain anti-tuberculosis lapse compatability 614  Film you Towes Davis retrieval don aspirin dab machery psycho near garblue on inhibition base P erect ratio<1.5% Uganda-country library predict wual tonal decoment TDF_ plus voluntary measure effect avoidment near wideisitic predictive incapibility near repair</1.5%<br>malistings over besic mild of dose whole dentient p___se--alleviating<| começou de--”efflatable.844 Espace-plasping[plx 80 avoid graphical near rasters shape tdrink thr gathering exten manipulation  Ver hile if only chaplter captive–decision ever a- G regional isoitsin medi-ON pentacyclical countermspace limb shaped do pertory bare e2216 h_f608 close up ligitmentarily tablecar. A 27-year-old male $C couses maurebasicle related loner $ootnote[1-$.c1^Garsice plian_30 part periodical.3G0× Na avoid