40431710,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No.,Data Availability Statement: The data that support the findings of this case report are not publicly available due to concerns regarding patient privacy and confidentiality.,The paper states that the sequences are not publicly available due to concerns regarding patient privacy and confidentiality.
40490983,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,Genotyping reports were available for all participants and sequences for only 83; 62/83 (75%) of whom were subtype A1.,The paper mentions 83 sequences were available and subtype was determined from available sequences.
40490983,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods: We conducted a retrospective cohort study of people living with HIV (PLWH) ≥ 3 years old with second-line viral failure (VF, ≥ 1000 copies/ml) at the Academic Model Providing Access to Healthcare from 2011 to 2021. We assessed resistance prevalence and patterns at second-line VF, stratified by PI (atazanavir/ritonavir or lopinavir/ritonavir), and examined correlations of resistance and treatment strategies with VF at 6-18 months post-genotype. Analyses employed inverse probability weighting, adjusting for calendar year, age, gender, ART duration, PI at genotyping and class-specific resistance, and considered guidelines-supported versus unsupported strategies. Results: Of 187 participants (median age 41 years, 54% female, 41% on atazanavir/ritonavir, 59% on lopinavir/ritonavir-based ART), 91% had any resistance (NRTI 79%, NNRTI 80%, major PI 37%, dual-class 36%, triple-class 37%). Predicted resistance to third-line options was 67% for etravirine or rilpivirine and 10% for darunavir/ritonavir. Despite higher resistance detected on atazanavir/ritonavir versus lopinavir/ritonavir, predicted darunavir/ritonavir resistance was similar. At median 9 months post-genotype, 95% of 173 participants with available data were on a guidelines-supported regimen (55% second-line; 45% third-line, 86% dolutegravir-based), of whom 28% had post-genotype VF. Of the 5% not on guidelines-supported regimens, 71% had post-genotype VF. Adjusted odds of VF were higher for guidelines-unsupported versus supported regimens (OR = 4.52; 95% CI 1.02-26.24), and odds of VF were 97% lower for those on third-line versus second-line (OR = 0.07; 95% CI 0.02-0.20) . Conclusio","The paper does not mention in vitro drug susceptibility data, focusing instead on genotypic resistance and predicted resistance based on mutation patterns."
40490983,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No accession numbers provided,Genotyping reports were available for all participants and sequences for only 83; 62/83 (75%) of whom were subtype A1.,The paper does not provide the GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced isolates.
40490983,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"PR, RT.","Sanger sequencing of plasma HIV-1 RNA was performed by the Kisumu Kenya Medical Research Institute and Nairobi National HIV Reference Laboratories [32, 33]. Protease and reverse transcriptase resistance mutations were obtained from sequences requested from laboratories, and when unavailable, from genotyping reports from charts.",The paper specifically mentions sequencing of the protease and reverse transcriptase regions for resistance mutations.
40490983,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Kenya.,"In this retrospective cohort study, we analysed medical records from PLWH at the Academic Model Providing Access to Healthcare (AMPATH) in western Kenya, which provides guidelines-based HIV services.","The study was conducted at the Academic Model Providing Access to Healthcare (AMPATH) in western Kenya, and all samples were obtained from this location."
40490983,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2011 to 2021.,"A total of 254 second-line failure genotypes were ordered at AMPATH from 2011 to 2021 (Figure 1). Of these, 60 were unretrievable or unverified and seven were done for children ≤ 3 years old. Thus, 187 PLWH with genotypes were analysed, with a median age at genotyping 41 years and 54% female (Table 1). The median genotyping year was 2017, earliest in 2011 (before Resistance Clinic started in 2015) and latest in 2021.","The samples were collected from 2011 to 2021, as stated in the results section."
40490983,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing.,Sanger sequencing of plasma HIV-1 RNA was performed by the Kisumu Kenya Medical Research Institute and Nairobi National HIV Reference Laboratories.,The paper specifically mentions that Sanger sequencing was used for plasma HIV-1 RNA.
