40490983,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma RNA.,Sanger sequencing of plasma HIV-1 RNA was performed by the Kisumu Kenya Medical Research Institute and Nairobi National HIV Reference Laboratories.,The paper specifies that plasma HIV-1 RNA was used for sequencing.
40490983,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Methods: We conducted a retrospective cohort study of people living with HIV (PLWH) ≥ 3 years old with second-line viral failure (VF, ≥ 1000 copies/ml) at the Academic Model Providing Access to Healthcare from 2011 to 2021. We assessed resistance prevalence and patterns at second-line VF, stratified by PI (atazanavir/ritonavir or lopinavir/ritonavir), and examined correlations of resistance and treatment strategies with VF at 6-18 months post-genotype. Analyses employed inverse probability weighting, adjusting for calendar year, age, gender, ART duration, PI at genotyping and class-specific resistance, and considered guidelines-supported versus unsupported strategies.","The paper mentions the stud is about second-line viral failure (VF, ≥ 1000 copies/ml), which indicates some participants experience virological failure (VF) on a treatment regimen (second-line)."
40490983,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Methods: We conducted a retrospective cohort study of people living with HIV (PLWH) ≥ 3 years old with second-line viral failure (VF, ≥ 1000 copies/ml) at the Academic Model Providing Access to Healthcare from 2011 to 2021. We assessed resistance prevalence and patterns at second-line VF, stratified by PI (atazanavir/ritonavir or lopinavir/ritonavir), and examined correlations of resistance and treatment strategies with VF at 6-18 months post-genotype. Analyses employed inverse probability weighting, adjusting for calendar year, age, gender, ART duration, PI at genotyping and class-specific resistance, and considered guidelines-supported versus unsupported strategies.","The paper describes a retrospective cohort study, not a clinical trial."
