40839365,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We enrolled consenting adults (≥15 years), on DBR for ≥9 months, when returning for follow-up VL testing after a previous high VL (≥1000 copies/mL) and completing IAC. Enrolled PLHIV submitted plasma samples for VL testing and those with VL ≥1000 copies/mL (confirmed virologic failure) underwent genotypic HIVDR testing. Among 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped for protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase.","The paper reports that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples. Specifically, it mentions that samples were genotyped for protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase from participants with confirmed virologic failure."
40839365,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Results: Among 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped for protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase. Prevalence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) to nucleotide reverse-transcriptase inhibitors was 22.6% (95% CI: 17.4% to 27.8%), to non-nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitors 55.1% (95% CI 48.2% to 62.1%), and to protease inhibitors 4.9% (95% CI 1.3% to 8.5%). Prevalence of major dolutegravir DRMs was 8.5% (95% CI 5.3% to 11.9%). DTG DRMs identified included G118R (8), E138K (8), R263K/A (5), Q148R (3), and T66A/I/V (3).",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing. It focuses on genotypic resistance testing and the prevalence of resistance mutations in patient samples.
40839365,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
40839365,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Protease, Reverse Transcriptase, Integrase","Among 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped for protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase.","The paper clearly states that the genes sequenced included protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase."
40839365,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Malawi,HIV Drug Resistance to Dolutegravir Among Adults Investigated for Antiretroviral Treatment Failure in Malawi: A 2023 National Cross-Sectional Survey,"The study was conducted in Malawi, and all samples were obtained from this country."
40839365,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2022-2023,The survey was conducted between July 2022 and February 2023.,"The Methods section states that the survey was conducted between July 2022 and February 2023, indicating the years from which the samples were obtained."
40839365,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"At NHRL, VL was measured using the automated Abbott m2000sp/rt Real Time HIV-1 assay from 200 μL of plasma. Samples with VL ≥1000 copies/mL underwent genotypic analysis. HIV RNA was extracted using NUCLISENS easyMAG (BioMerieux) from 200 μL of plasma, with 10 μL of extracted RNA; gene fragments of protease and reverse transcriptase and integrase were amplified and sequenced using HIV-1 Genotyping kit with Integrase and the 3500XL Genetic Analyzers (Applied Biosystems). Sanger sequence electropherograms were analyzed by using RECall (v2.32)—Web-based sequence analysis software (https://recall.bccfe.ca/account/login).","The paper describes the use of the 3500XL Genetic Analyzers (Applied Biosystems) and Sanger sequence electropherograms, which are indicative of Sanger sequencing."
40839365,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
40839365,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Enrolled PLHIV submitted plasma samples for VL testing and those with VL ≥1000 copies/mL (confirmed virologic failure) underwent genotypic HIVDR testing. Plasma was separated from the venous whole blood samples that were collected in an EDTA tube by centrifugation and aliquoted at each health facility and kept at −20°C until they were transported on dry ice to the National HIV Reference Laboratory (NHRL) in Lilongwe. At NHRL, VL was measured using the automated Abbott m2000sp/rt Real Time HIV-1 assay from 200 μL of plasma. Samples with VL ≥1000 copies/mL underwent genotypic analysis. HIV RNA was extracted using NUCLISENS easyMAG (BioMerieux) from 200 μL of plasma, with 10 μL of extracted RNA; gene fragments of protease and reverse transcriptase and integrase were amplified and sequenced using HIV-1 Genotyping kit with Integrase and the 3500XL Genetic Analyzers (Applied Biosystems).",The paper specifies that plasma samples were used for VL testing and subsequently for genotypic HIVDR testing.
40839365,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Enrolled PLHIV submitted plasma samples for VL testing and those with VL ≥1000 copies/mL (confirmed virologic failure) underwent genotypic HIVDR testing. Among 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped for protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase. Prevalence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) to nucleotide reverse-transcriptase inhibitors was 22.6% (95% CI: 17.4% to 27.8%), to non-nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitors 55.1% (95% CI 48.2% to 62.1%), and to protease inhibitors 4.9% (95% CI 1.3% to 8.5%). Prevalence of major dolutegravir DRMs was 8.5% (95% CI 5.3% to 11.9%). DTG DRMs identified included G118R (8), E138K (8), R263K/A (5), Q148R (3), and T66A/I/V (3).","The paper mentions that samples were obtained from individuals with confirmed virologic failure, indicating they were on a treatment regimen."
40839365,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"A cross-sectional design was used to assess drug resistance among adult PLHIV with virologic nonsuppression in Malawi between July 2022 and February 2023. Eligible participants were PLHIV aged 15 years or older, on a DBR for at least 9 months, latest VL was ≥1000 copies/mL, and had completed at least 1 session of IAC per national guidelines.[16] A routine VL test in the Malawi HIV treatment program is required 6 months after ART initiation and every 12 months thereafter; for breastfeeding women and young children, a routine VL is every 6 months. A targeted VL is required if person on treatment with a routine ≥1000 copies/mL received IAC and at least 3 months since IAC had started. During IAC, a trained counselor or experienced ART provider has an individual session with a client on ART, to analyze any circumstances that may contribute to reduced adherence and to find feasible solutions for these issues together with the client. Drug resistance testing is conducted if the targeted VL is ≥1000 copies/mL and 3 months after starting IAC. The participants were identified when returning for a targeted VL and provided written informed consent or assent appropriately (see ethics review below). The survey did not deviate from normal sample collection procedures outlines in the HIV treatment guidelines.[17] If such adults had a second VL test result ≥1000 copies/mL, they are coined as having confirmed virologic failure and were offered enrolment for HIVDR testing in this survey.","The paper describes a national survey and not a clinical trial, focusing on drug resistance assessment among adults with virologic nonsuppression."
40839365,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,212,"Among 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped for protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase.",The paper states that 212 samples were successfully genotyped from participants with confirmed virologic failure.
40839365,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"HIV Drug Resistance to Dolutegravir Among Adults Investigated for Antiretroviral Treatment Failure in Malawi: A 2023 National Cross-Sectional Survey. A cross-sectional design was used to assess drug resistance among adult PLHIV with virologic nonsuppression in Malawi between July 2022 and February 2023. Eligible participants were PLHIV aged 15 years or older, on a DBR for at least 9 months, latest VL was ≥1000 copies/mL, and had completed at least 1 session of IAC per national guidelines.  
Stanford University HIVDR Database (version 9.5) was used to interpret results.","The paper mentions the study population are people living with HIV (PLHIV) who had a dolutegravir-based regimen (DBR) and had virological failure after intensive counseling, indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals who had received ARV drugs."
40839365,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI","The survey was conducted between July 2022 and February 2023. We randomly selected 18 from 25 highest volume ART clinics in Malawi. We enrolled consenting adults (≥15 years), on DBR for ≥9 months, when returning for follow-up VL testing after a previous high VL (≥1000 copies/mL) and completing IAC. Enrolled PLHIV submitted plasma samples for VL testing and those with VL ≥1000 copies/mL (confirmed virologic failure) underwent genotypic HIVDR testing. Stanford University HIVDR Database (version 9.5) was used to interpret results. 

HIV Drug Resistance to Dolutegravir Among Adults Investigated for Antiretroviral Treatment Failure in Malawi: A 2023 National Cross-Sectional Survey. A cross-sectional design was used to assess drug resistance among adult PLHIV with virologic nonsuppression in Malawi between July 2022 and February 2023. Eligible participants were PLHIV aged 15 years or older, on a DBR for at least 9 months, latest VL was ≥1000 copies/mL, and had completed at least 1 session of IAC per national guidelines. A routine VL test in the Malawi HIV treatment program is required 6 months after ART initiation and every 12 months thereafter; for breastfeeding women and young children, a routine VL is every 6 months.  
Abstract:  In Malawi, 95.6% of adult people living with HIV (PLHIV) on antiretroviral therapy (ART) use dolutegravir-based regimens (DBR), however, prevalence of dolutegravir resistance is unknown. We conducted a national HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) survey in adults on DBR with high viral load (VL) results after intensive adherence counseling (IAC).  

Among 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped for protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase. Prevalence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) to nucleotide reverse-transcriptase inhibitors was 22.6% (95% CI: 17.4% to 27.8%), to non-nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitors 55.1% (95% CI 48.2% to 62.1%), and to protease inhibitors 4.9% (95% CI 1.3% to 8.5%). Prevalence of major dolutegravir DRMs was 8.5% (95% CI 5.3% to 11.9%). DTG DRMs identified included G118R (8), E138K (8), R263K/A (5), Q148R (3), and T66A/I/V (3).","The paper mentions that participants were on a dolutegravir-based regimen and were tested for drug resistance mutations to NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs."
40839365,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Abacavir, Emtricitabine, Lamivudine, Stavudine, Tenofovir, Efavirenz, Nevirapine, Darunavir, Raltegravir, Dolutegravir","For resistance to individual NRTIs, prevalence of low, intermediate, or high resistance was most common for abacavir (20.1%) followed by emtricitabine (18.2%), lamivudine (16.9%), stavudine (12.8%), and tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (10.3%). For individual NNRTIs, low, intermediate, or high resistance to efavirenz and nevirapine was equally high (49.5%).  For INSTIs, resistance to cabotegravir was 9.4% and to DTG was 8.5% (95% CI: 5.3% to 11.9%).","The paper lists specific NRTIs (Abacavir, Emtricitabine, Lamivudine, Stavudine, Tenofovir), NNRTIs (Efavirenz, Nevirapine), PIs (Darunavir), INSTIs (Raltegravir and Dolutegravir), that were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing."
40839365,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention whether the sequences obtained were made publicly available.
40857111,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"-Methods: After the 96-week randomized phase of ALLIANCE, participants could enter a 48-week open-label B/F/TAF-only phase. This subanalysis reports HIV-1 resistance results to the end of masked treatment (DTG+F/TDF, n=122) and to study end (B/F/TAF, n=119; DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF, n=89). Baseline resistance was determined using historical and screening genotypes of protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN), if available. Participants with virologic failure had postbaseline resistance testing of PR/RT/IN; those subsequently not achieving virologic suppression (VS; HIV-1 RNA <50 copies/mL) were included in final resistance analysis population (RAP). Results: Baseline resistance mutations were uncommon and did not affect VS rates. Criteria for final RAP inclusion were met by 7, 6, and 1 participant in the DTG+F/TDF, B/F/TAF, and DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF groups, respectively. For participants with genotyping data, no treatment-emergent primary resistance mutations to study drugs developed. From 8 participants who had resistance testing but did not qualify for inclusion in final RAP, 1 participant on DTG + F/TDF with documented nonadherence had emergent resistance mutations to tenofovir and emtricitabine and subsequently achieved VS.","The paper discusses the resistance testing of HIV-1 from participants in the study, indicating that sequences were obtained from patient samples to analyze resistance mutations."
40857111,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"This subanalysis reports HIV-1 resistance results to the end of masked treatment (DTG+F/TDF, n=122) and to study end (B/F/TAF, n=119; DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF, n=89). Baseline resistance was determined using historical and screening genotypes of protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN), if available. Participants with virologic failure had postbaseline resistance testing of PR/RT/IN; those subsequently not achieving virologic suppression (VS; HIV-1 RNA <50 copies/mL) were included in final resistance analysis population (RAP). Results: Baseline resistance mutations were uncommon and did not affect VS rates. Criteria for final RAP inclusion were met by 7, 6, and 1 participant in the DTG+F/TDF, B/F/TAF, and DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF groups, respectively. For participants with genotyping data, no treatment-emergent primary resistance mutations to study drugs developed. From 8 participants who had resistance testing but did not qualify for inclusion in final RAP, 1 participant on DTG + F/TDF with documented nonadherence had emergent resistance mutations to tenofovir and emtricitabine and subsequently achieved VS.","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data, focusing instead on genotypic resistance analysis from patient samples."
40857111,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not provided,No evidence,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
40857111,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Protease (PR), Reverse Transcriptase (RT), Integrase (IN)","Baseline resistance was determined using historical and screening genotypes of protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN), if available. Participants with virologic failure had postbaseline resistance testing of PR/RT/IN; those subsequently not achieving virologic suppression (VS; HIV-1 RNA <50 copies/mL) were included in final resistance analysis population (RAP).","The paper mentions sequencing and resistance testing of the protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes for resistance mutations."
40857111,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not specified,No evidence,The paper does not specify the countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained.
40857111,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not specified,No evidence,The paper does not provide information on the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained.
40857111,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Not specified,No evidence,The paper does not specify the sequencing method used for obtaining the HIV sequences.
40857111,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,Not mentioned,No evidence,The paper does not mention whether samples were cloned prior to sequencing.
40857111,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Not specified,No evidence,The paper does not specify the type of clinical specimens that were sequenced.
40857111,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,Participants with virologic failure had postbaseline resistance testing of PR/RT/IN; those subsequently not achieving virologic suppression (VS; HIV-1 RNA <50 copies/mL) were included in final resistance analysis population (RAP).,The paper mentions that resistance testing was performed on participants with virological failure.
40857111,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,The design of the phase 3 randomized ALLIANCE study (GS-US-380-4458; NCT03547908) has been described previously.,"The paper refers to the study as a phase 3 randomized clinical trial, indicating that the patients were part of a clinical trial."
40857111,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,208,"In total, 208 participants received ≥1 dose of B/F/TAF in either phase and had ≥1 on-treatment, postbaseline HIV-1 RNA and HBV DNA result; 119 of these participants only received B/F/TAF while 89 received DTG+F/TDF in the randomized phase, then received ≥1 dose of B/F/TAF in the OLE phase.","The paper states that 208 participants had resistance testing and genotyping data available, which indicates they had samples obtained for HIV sequencing."
40857111,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"In total, 208 participants received ≥1 dose of B/F/TAF in either phase and had ≥1 on-treatment, postbaseline HIV-1 RNA and HBV DNA result; 119 of these participants only received B/F/TAF while 89 received DTG+F/TDF in the randomized phase, then received ≥1 dose of B/F/TAF in the OLE phase.","The study involved participants who received either B/F/TAF or DTG+F/TDF, both of which are antiretroviral (ARV) drugs, indicating that sequences were obtained from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs."
40857111,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI (Nucleoside Reverse Transcriptase Inhibitors), NNRTI (Non-Nucleoside Reverse Transcriptase Inhibitors), PI (Protease Inhibitors), INSTI (Integrase Strand Transfer Inhibitors)","In brief, participants were aged ≥18 years, with HIV-1 RNA ≥500 copies/mL and HBV DNA ≥2000 IU/mL. After the 96-week randomized phase of ALLIANCE, participants could enter a 48-week open-label B/F/TAF-only phase. This subanalysis reports HIV-1 resistance results to the end of masked treatment (DTG+F/TDF, n=122) and to study end (B/F/TAF, n=119; DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF, n=89). Baseline resistance was determined using historical and screening genotypes of protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN), if available. Participants with virologic failure had postbaseline resistance testing of PR/RT/IN; those subsequently not achieving virologic suppression (VS; HIV-1 RNA <50 copies/mL) were included in final resistance analysis population (RAP). Results: Baseline resistance mutations were uncommon and did not affect VS rates. Criteria for final RAP inclusion were met by 7, 6, and 1 participant in the DTG+F/TDF, B/F/TAF, and DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF groups, respectively. For participants with genotyping data, no treatment-emergent primary resistance mutations to study drugs developed. From 8 participants who had resistance testing but did not qualify for inclusion in final RAP, 1 participant on DTG + F/TDF with documented nonadherence had emergent resistance mutations to tenofovir and emtricitabine and subsequently achieved VS","The paper mentions the use of NRTI, NNRTI, PI,  INSTI class drugs in the study."
40857111,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Bictegravir (BIC), Emtricitabine (FTC), Tenofovir Alafenamide (TAF), Dolutegravir (DTG), Tenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate (TDF)","After the 96-week randomized phase of ALLIANCE, participants could enter a 48-week open-label B/F/TAF-only phase. This subanalysis reports HIV-1 resistance results to the end of masked treatment (DTG+F/TDF, n=122) and to study end (B/F/TAF, n=119; DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF, n=89).","The paper lists the specific drugs used in the study, including bictegravir, emtricitabine, tenofovir alafenamide, dolutegravir, and tenofovir disoproxil fumarate."
40857111,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention whether the sequences obtained were made publicly available.
40872801,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The emergence and spread of HIV drug resistance mutations (DRMs) pose a threat to current and future treatment options. To inform policy, this review aimed to determine the magnitude and patterns of DRMs in patients on ART in Tanzania. A systematic literature search was conducted in MEDLINE through PubMed, Embase, and CINAHL up to December 2024. A total of 9685 HIV patients from 23 eligible studies were analyzed. The prevalence of virological failure in studies that used a threshold of  >1000  and  >400  copies/mL was  24.83%  (95% CI: 17.85-32.53%) and  36.94%  (95% CI: 24.79-50.00%), respectively. Major DRMs were observed at  87.61%  (95% CI: 76.25-95.91%). A decrease in prevalence was observed in studies conducted from 2019, with a pooled prevalence of  62.15%  (95% CI: 31.57-88.33%). The most frequently observed HIV-1 subtypes were subtype C at  36.20%  (95% CI: 30.71-41.85%), A1 at  33.13%  (95% CI: 28.23-38.20%), and subtype D at  16.00%  (95% CI: 11.41-21.12%), while recombinant forms of the virus were observed at  13.29%  (95% CI: 9.79-17.17%). The prevalence of DRMs against NRTIs and NNRTIs was significantly high, while that against INSTIs and PIs was low, supporting the continued use of PI- and INSTI-based regimens in Tanzania and the need for continued surveillance of DRMs.","The paper does not provide any data regarding HIV sequences from patient samples, as it focuses on a systematic review and meta-analysis of existing studies."
40872801,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The emergence and spread of HIV drug resistance mutations (DRMs) pose a threat to current and future treatment options. To inform policy, this review aimed to determine the magnitude and patterns of DRMs in patients on ART in Tanzania. A systematic literature search was conducted in MEDLINE through PubMed, Embase, and CINAHL up to December 2024. A total of 9685 HIV patients from 23 eligible studies were analyzed. The prevalence of virological failure in studies that used a threshold of  >1000  and  >400  copies/mL was  24.83%  (95% CI: 17.85-32.53%) and  36.94%  (95% CI: 24.79-50.00%), respectively. Major DRMs were observed at  87.61%  (95% CI: 76.25-95.91%). A decrease in prevalence was observed in studies conducted from 2019, with a pooled prevalence of  62.15%  (95% CI: 31.57-88.33%). The most frequently observed HIV-1 subtypes were subtype C at  36.20%  (95% CI: 30.71-41.85%), A1 at  33.13%  (95% CI: 28.23-38.20%), and subtype D at  16.00%  (95% CI: 11.41-21.12%), while recombinant forms of the virus were observed at  13.29%  (95% CI: 9.79-17.17%). The prevalence of DRMs against NRTIs and NNRTIs was significantly high, while that against INSTIs and PIs was low, supporting the continued use of PI- and INSTI-based regimens in Tanzania and the need for continued surveillance of DRMs.","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data, focusing instead on the prevalence and patterns of drug resistance mutations."
