40938120,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"A total of 1,048 eligible pol sequences were analyzed.","A total of 1,048 eligible pol sequences were analyzed.","The study involved analyzing HIV sequences from patient samples, specifically the pol gene sequences from plasma samples of newly diagnosed individuals."
40938120,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No.,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility data, focusing instead on the prevalence and transmission of drug resistance among patient samples."
40938120,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,HIV-1 sequence data generated in this study have been deposited in GenBank and are available under accession numbers PX133190-PX134237.,HIV-1 sequence data generated in this study have been deposited in GenBank and are available under accession numbers PX133190-PX134237.,The paper explicitly states the GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
40938120,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"The pol gene, covering the full-length protease and the first 299 codons of the RT gene.","An in-house RT nested PCR was used to amplify a 1,061 bp fragment of the pol gene, covering the full-length protease (PR, 99 codons) and the first 299 codons of the RT gene.","The laboratory testing section describes the sequencing of the pol gene, which includes the protease and reverse transcriptase regions."
40938120,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China.,"This study assessed the prevalence, associated factors, and transmission networks of PDR among newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals in Guangxi, China, from 2019 to 2022.","The study was conducted in Nanning City, Guangxi Province, China, and the samples were obtained from this region."
40938120,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2019 to 2022.,"A cross-sectional study was conducted between 2019 and 2022 in Nanning, Guangxi, involving 1,260 newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals with HIV.",The study covered the years 2019 to 2022 for collecting and analyzing the samples.
40938120,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing.,"PCR products were confirmed by agarose gel electrophoresis, and positive amplicons were sequenced by Sangon Biotech (Shanghai, China) using the Applied Biosystems 3730XL Genetic Analyzer.","The paper mentions the use of the Applied Biosystems 3730XL Genetic Analyzer, which is a platform used for Sanger sequencing."
40938120,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No.,No evidence,"The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, indicating direct sequencing of PCR products."
40938120,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples.,Venous blood samples (10 mL) were collected from each participant. Plasma was separated by centrifugation and stored in aliquots at −80 °C for further analysis. HIV-1 RNA was extracted from plasma samples using the High Pure Viral RNA Kit.,The laboratory testing section specifies that plasma samples were collected and used for RNA extraction and subsequent sequencing.
40938120,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No.,"Molecular epidemiology and pretreatment drug resistance of HIV-1 among newly diagnosed individuals in Nanning City, Guangxi, China  This study assessed the prevalence, associated factors, and transmission networks of PDR among newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals in Guangxi, China, from 2019 to 2022. A cross-sectional study was conducted between 2019 and 2022 in Nanning, Guangxi, involving 1,260 newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals with HIV.","The participants were newly diagnosed and ART-naive, so they hadn’t experienced virological failure on a regimen."
40938120,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No.,The study was performed on newly diagnosed AIDS patients in routine clinics.,The study was a cross-sectional survey and the patients were from routine clinics.
40938120,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,"1,260 newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals.","A cross-sectional study was conducted between 2019 and 2022 in Nanning, Guangxi, involving 1,260 newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals with HIV. To construct the molecular transmission network, we recruited more than 60% newly diagnosed HIV patients each year.","The study enrolled 1,260 newly diagnosed individuals, from whom samples were obtained for sequencing."
40938120,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No.,"Molecular epidemiology and pretreatment drug resistance of HIV-1 among newly diagnosed individuals in Nanning City, Guangxi, China  This study assessed the prevalence, associated factors, and transmission networks of PDR among newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals in Guangxi, China, from 2019 to 2022. A cross-sectional study was conducted between 2019 and 2022 in Nanning, Guangxi, involving 1,260 newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals with HIV.","The participants were newly diagnosed and ART-naive, indicating they had not previously received ARV drugs."
40938120,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None.,"Molecular epidemiology and pretreatment drug resistance of HIV-1 among newly diagnosed individuals in Nanning City, Guangxi, China  This study assessed the prevalence, associated factors, and transmission networks of PDR among newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals in Guangxi, China, from 2019 to 2022. A cross-sectional study was conducted between 2019 and 2022 in Nanning, Guangxi, involving 1,260 newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals with HIV.","The study focused on newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals, meaning they had not received any drug classes before sequencing."
40938120,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None.,"Molecular epidemiology and pretreatment drug resistance of HIV-1 among newly diagnosed individuals in Nanning City, Guangxi, China  This study assessed the prevalence, associated factors, and transmission networks of PDR among newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals in Guangxi, China, from 2019 to 2022. A cross-sectional study was conducted between 2019 and 2022 in Nanning, Guangxi, involving 1,260 newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals with HIV.","The study focused on newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals, meaning they had not received any drugs before sequencing."
40938120,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes.,HIV-1 sequence data generated in this study have been deposited in GenBank and are available under accession numbers PX133190-PX134237.,The paper explicitly states that the HIV-1 sequence data generated in the study have been deposited in GenBank.
40938996,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"We find that in primary peripheral blood mononuclear cells, HIV-1 acquires mutations in the viral envelope glycoprotein, NC, and occasionally IN during selection for INSTI resistance.",The paper discusses mutations selected in the presence of the INSTI dolutegravir in various HIV-1 strains but does not mention sequences obtained from patient samples. It focuses on laboratory studies and primary cell cultures rather than clinical samples from individuals.
40938996,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"Consistent with the multicycle replication data, the selected NC mutations, except for NC-R29G, confer ∼2.8- to 7.8-fold resistance to DTG. We note that the fold resistance measured in the spreading infection (Fig. 1A) and the single-round infection (Fig. 1C) are somewhat different, likely reflecting different assay formats and differences in the virus used [e.g., full-length, replication-competent virus in Fig. 1A versus single-round reporter virus in Fig. 1C]. To assess DTG resistance in a more physiologically relevant cell type, we examined DTG sensitivity of the NC mutants in primary CD4+ T cells (Fig. 1, E and F). Although some donor-dependent variability was observed, all of the NC mutants tested here increased DTG IC50 in primary CD4+ T cells by ∼2- to 6-fold.","The paper includes data on drug susceptibility, such as the inhibition concentrations of differently selected mutants and the wild-type NL4-3 virus, although such data is not always presented or referred to in the tests described within the results."
40938996,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not mention GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on the mechanisms of resistance and the experimental details of the studies conducted.
40938996,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"NC, IN","We previously reported that mutations in the viral nucleocapsid (NC) are selected in the presence of the INSTI dolutegravir (DTG). We find that in primary peripheral blood mononuclear cells, HIV-1 acquires mutations in the viral envelope glycoprotein, NC, and occasionally IN during selection for INSTI resistance.","The paper reports mutations in the nucleocapsid (NC) and integrase (IN) genes, indicating that these genes were sequenced to identify the mutations associated with INSTI resistance."
40938996,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not provide information about the geographical origins of the sequenced samples. It focuses on the laboratory studies and experiments conducted with specific HIV-1 strains.
40938996,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not mention the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained. It primarily discusses the experimental results and the implications of the findings.
40938996,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,The PCR amplicon was purified and cloned into pCR-Blunt II-TOPO vector (Thermo Fisher Scientific) per the manufacturer's instructions. The 2-LTR junction sequence was determined by Sanger sequencing (Poochon Scientific) using the M13 reverse primer.,"The paper mentions that the 2-LTR junction sequence was determined by Sanger sequencing, indicating that this method was used for sequencing the samples in the study."
40938996,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,The PCR amplicon was purified and cloned into pCR-Blunt II-TOPO vector (Thermo Fisher Scientific) per the manufacturer's instructions.,The paper did not mention that samples were cloned prior to sequencing. It mentions that the samples were purified and cloned into vectors for sequencing.
40938996,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Cell culture supernatants,"The NC regions with the indicated mutations were cloned into the pNL4-3/KFS using Spe I and Sbf I restriction sites. NC-H23C and H44C (30) were introduced into pNL N-23 ∆K.  In contrast, the control mutants NC-H44C, IN-D116N, and 3′ PPT-2C3A5T6Δ failed to replicate in the SupT1 T cell line. We next examined the Nef protein. PBMCs were isolated using a ficoll gradient and stimulated with phytohemagglutinin (PHA-P; 2 μg/ml) for 3 to 5 days before infection and then cultured in interleukin-2 (IL-2; 50 U/ml). Primary CD4+ T cells isolated from PBMCs. Activated PBMCs were infected with the indicated viruses to initiate the passaging experiments. At the time points indicated by the arrows, genomic DNA was extracted from the drug-treated cultures.","The paper mentions the used of PBMC isolates  stimulated and non stimulated PBMC, and SupT1 cells, and the cell culture supernatants were used for sequencing."
40938996,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,We previously reported that mutations in the viral nucleocapsid (NC) are selected in the presence of the INSTI dolutegravir (DTG).,The paper does not mention sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure. It primarily discusses mutations selected in vitro and in cell culture systems.
40938996,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We previously reported that mutations in the viral nucleocapsid (NC) are selected in the presence of the INSTI dolutegravir (DTG). Here, we show that these NC mutations accelerate the kinetics of viral DNA integration, suggesting that they limit the window of time available for INSTIs to block viral DNA integration. We find that in primary peripheral blood mononuclear cells, HIV-1 acquires mutations in the viral envelope glycoprotein, NC, and occasionally IN during selection for INSTI resistance. Notably, the selected NC and IN mutations act in concert to reduce the susceptibility of the virus to INSTIs. These results provide insights into the mechanism by which HIV-1 escapes the inhibitory activity of INSTIs and underscore the importance of genotypic analysis outside IN in PWH experiencing VF on INSTI-containing drug regimens.","The paper discusses laboratory studies and experiments on HIV-1 strains, not clinical trials involving patients. It focuses on the mechanisms of resistance and the selection of mutations."
40938996,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,Not reported,"We find that in primary peripheral blood mononuclear cells, HIV-1 acquires mutations in the viral envelope glycoprotein, NC, and occasionally IN during selection for INSTI resistance.",The paper does not mention the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for sequencing. It focuses on laboratory studies and experimental results.
40938996,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"Here, we show that these NC mutations accelerate the kinetics of viral DNA integration, suggesting that they limit the window of time available for INSTIs to block viral DNA integration. Persons with HIV (PWH) receiving integrase (IN) strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs) have been reported to experience virologic failure (VF) in the absence of resistance mutations in IN.",The paper does not mention sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs. It focuses on laboratory studies and selected HIV-1 strains.
40938996,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not reported,"Here, we show that these NC mutations accelerate the kinetics of viral DNA integration, suggesting that they limit the window of time available for INSTIs to block viral DNA integration. Persons with HIV (PWH) receiving integrase (IN) strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs) have been reported to experience virologic failure (VF) in the absence of resistance mutations in IN.",The paper does not provide information on drug classes received by individuals before sample sequencing. It primarily discusses mutations selected in vitro.
40938996,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not reported,"Here, we show that these NC mutations accelerate the kinetics of viral DNA integration, suggesting that they limit the window of time available for INSTIs to block viral DNA integration. Persons with HIV (PWH) receiving integrase (IN) strand transfer inhibitors (INSTIs) have been reported to experience virologic failure (VF) in the absence of resistance mutations in IN.",The paper does not mention specific drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing. It focuses on in vitro studies and the selection of mutations.
40938996,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention whether the sequences have been made publicly available. It focuses on the experimental results and the implications of the findings.
40965168,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Patient plasma samples were obtained from studies GS-US-380-1490, GS-US-292-0104, GS-US-200-4334, and GS-US-200-4625. The HIV-1 gag-protease DNA fragment from various HIV-1 isolates was cloned (pXXLAI vector), and viruses were generated by transfection. The HIV-1 gag-protease fragment from these plasma samples was amplified by two rounds of 30 cycles of PCR and subcloned into Zero Blunt TOPO pCR vector (Thermo Fisher, #450031).","The paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples, specifically mentioning the use of clinical isolates with diverse HIV-1 subtypes and the amplification and sequencing of HIV-1 gag-protease DNA from these samples."
40965168,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,Drug susceptibilities of the 24 clinical isolates were characterized in the MT-2 multicycle phenotypic assay. The overall mean LEN EC50 for all viral isolates was 0.067nmol/1 (standard deviation [SD]: 0.027).,"The paper includes in vitro drug susceptibility data, detailing the testing of clinical isolates for their susceptibility to LEN and other antiretroviral drugs in a multicycle phenotypic assay."
40965168,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,None,No GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates were reported in the paper.,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, focusing instead on the susceptibility and resistance data."
40965168,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Gag, protease, capsid, reverse transcriptase genes","The HIV-1 gag-protease DNA fragment from these plasma samples was amplified by two rounds of 30 cycles of PCR and subcloned into Zero Blunt TOPO pCR vector (Thermo Fisher, #450031). The capsid and reverse transcriptase sequence from the selected viruses were analyzed to determine the presence of resistance mutations.","The paper mentions the sequencing of the HIV-1 gag-protease DNA fragment, as well as the capsid and reverse transcriptase sequences from selected viruses to determine the presence of resistance mutations."
40965168,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,None,"Patient plasma samples were obtained from studies GS-US-380-1490, GS-US-292-0104, GS-US-200-4334, and GS-US-200-4625. Patient informed consent was obtained prior to the collection of the clinical samples used in this study.","The paper does not specify the countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained, only mentioning the studies they were collected from."
40965168,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,None,"Patient plasma samples were obtained from studies GS-US-380-1490, GS-US-292-0104, GS-US-200-4334, and GS-US-200-4625. Patient informed consent was obtained prior to the collection of the clinical samples used in this study.",The paper does not provide specific years during which the sequenced samples were obtained.
40965168,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"The transformed bacteria were grown overnight, and the plasmid DNA was purified and sequenced at ELIM Biopharmaceuticals (Hayward, California, USA) No GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates were reported in the paper.","The paper mentions the use of plasmid DNA purification and sequencing at ELIM, which suggests the use of Sanger sequencing method."
40965168,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,Yes,"The HIV-1 gag-protease fragment from these plasma samples was amplified by two rounds of 30 cycles of PCR and subcloned into Zero Blunt TOPO pCR vector (Thermo Fisher, #450031).","The paper describes the cloning of the HIV-1 gag-protease fragment into a vector for sequencing, indicating that the samples were cloned prior to sequencing."
40965168,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"HIV-1 RNA was extracted from 50μl of plasma using the Mag Max-96Viral RNA isolation kit (Thermo Fisher, Waltham, Massachusetts, USA, # AMB 18365) following manufacturer's instructions, and eluted in 90μl of buffer. The HIV-1 RNA was reverse transcribed into cDNA and amplified using the single-step Superscript IV One-Step RT PCR System (Thermo Fisher, # 12594100). HIV-1 DNA amplification was generated through two rounds of PCR (first round PCR primers: U563 [GCTTAAGCCTCAATAAAGCTTGCCTTG; HXB2 nucleotide (nt) 516 to 542] and RT155 [GGCCCAATTTTGAAATTTCCCTTCC; reverse complement of HXB2 nt 2678 to 2704]; second round PCR primers: U613-IF [ATCTC-TAGCAGTGGCGCCCGAACAGGGACTTGAAAG; HXB2 nt 625 to 660] and RC-5-HIV-Xma-IF [GGCCATCCATCCCGGGCTTTAATTTTACTGGTACAGTTTC; reverse complement of HXB2 nt 2565 to 2604]. Reactions were conducted in a 50μl volume containing 10μl of RNA, 25μl 2X Platinum Super Fi II PCR master mix (Thermo Fisher, #12368010), 2.5μl of 10μmol/1 primers each, 0.5μl of superscript IV RT, and 9.5μl of water.",The paper specifies that HIV-1 RNA was extracted from plasma samples and used for sequencing the HIV-1 gag-protease DNA fragment.
40965168,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"The plasma samples were from participants who were treatment naive to the capsid inhibitor class, had diverse HIV-1 subtypes (A, A1, AE, AG, B, C, D, F1, G, H), and had HIV-1 RNA ranging from 3130 to 647000 copies/ml.",The paper states that the plasma samples were from treatment-naive participants who had diverse HIV-1 subtypes and had HIV-1 RNA ranging from 3130 to 647000 copies/ml. No individuals with virological failure were reported in the paper.
40965168,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"Patient plasma samples were obtained from studies GS-US-380-1490, GS-US-292-0104, GS-US-200-4334, and GS-US-200-4625. Patient informed consent was obtained prior to the collection of the clinical samples used in this study.","The paper mentions that the patient plasma samples were obtained from specific studies, indicating that the patients were part of clinical trials."
40965168,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,24,"The gag-protease DNA fragment from a total of 24 patient samples was cloned into pXXLAI, including 15 samples with non-B HIV-1 subtype (HIV-1 subtypes A1=1, AE=2, AG=3, C=1, D=3, F1=2, G=2, H=1) and nine with HIV-1 B subtype.","The paper states that the gag-protease DNA fragment from a total of 24 patient samples was cloned into pXXLAI, indicating that samples were obtained from 24 individuals for HIV sequencing."
40965168,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"The plasma samples were from participants who were treatment naive to the capsid inhibitor class, had diverse HIV-1 subtypes (A, A1, AE, AG, B, C, D, F1, G, H), and had HIV-1 RNA ranging from 3130 to 647000 copies/ml",The paper specifies that the plasma samples were from treatment-naive participants who had diverse HIV-1 subtypes and had HIV-1 RNA ranging from 3130 to 647000 copies/ml. No individuals who had previously received ARV drugs were reported in the paper.
