41023944,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024. According to DREAM protocols, individuals with virological failure were enrolled in a six-month enhanced adherence support program; no ART regimen changes are made during the enhanced adherence support phase. A repeated viral load test was performed after this period, and those with persistent viremia (>1000 copies/mL) were eligible for genotypic resistance testing. Only individuals on DTG-containing regimens at the time of resistance testing were included in this analysis. Elsewhere we described the wider population of the individuals with elevated VL [9].","The paper discusses genotypic resistance testing performed on HIV-positive individuals, which implies the sequencing of HIV from patient samples to identify resistance mutations."
41023944,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods We conducted a retrospective observational study in five DREAM centers in Mozambique. HIV-positive individuals on DTG-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) with confirmed virological failure (HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL) between July 2022 and December 2024 were included. Participants underwent genotypic resistance testing after six months of enhanced adherence support. Resistance mutations were identified via Sanger sequencing and interpreted using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database.
Results Of 28 individual tested, 13 (46.4%) exhibited DTG resistance (12/13 with intermediate or high-level resistance); 92.3% harboured HIV-1 subtype C. Frequently observed mutations included G118R (43.8%), E138K (43.8%), L74M (31.3%), and R263K (25.0%), often in combination. All DTG-resistant individuals who underwent testing for other drug classes (n = 9) also showed co-resistance to NRTIs and/or NNRTIs. Notably, high-level resistance emerged also in 6 failing participants having shifted to DTG while virologically suppressed.","The study focuses on genotypic resistance testing and the presence of resistance mutations in patient samples, rather than providing in vitro susceptibility data for drugs."
41023944,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence,"The paper does not mention GenBank accession numbers, which are typically provided for sequenced isolates."
41023944,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Integrase, Reverse Transcriptase, Protease","Resistance mutations were identified in the integrase region, and, when available, in the reverse transcriptase and protease regions.","The paper mentions that resistance mutations were identified in the integrase region, and, when available, in the reverse transcriptase and protease regions."
41023944,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Mozambique,"This is a retrospective observational study conducted across five centers of the DREAM program in Mozambique, including two centers in Maputo City, one in Maputo Province, and two in Sofala Province.","The study was conducted in Mozambique, and all samples were obtained from this country."
41023944,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2023-2024,"We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024.","The samples were obtained from 2023 to 2024, as stated in the study timeline."
41023944,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"Sequencing of the HIV integrase region was performed using the Sanger method at INQABA Biotechnical Industries, South Africa.",The paper explicitly states that sequencing of the HIV integrase region was performed using the Sanger method.
41023944,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
41023944,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"Plasma samples from participants with confirmed virological failure were collected and sent to the Zimpeto DREAM laboratory, where viral RNA was extracted and amplified using an in-house reverse transcription and nested PCR method.",The paper states that plasma samples from participants with confirmed virological failure were collected for sequencing.
41023944,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024.","The study included individuals who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL."
41023944,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We conducted a retrospective observational study in five DREAM centers in Mozambique. This is a retrospective observational study conducted across five centers of the DREAM program in Mozambique, including two centers in Maputo City, one in Maputo Province, and two in Sofala Province. The DREAM program is a community-based, primary care HIV initiative that integrates clinical follow-up, laboratory monitoring, and social support, and has been active in Mozambique since 2002.","The study was conducted as a retrospective observational study, not a clinical trial."
41023944,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,28 individuals,"In the considered period, 28 individuals on DTG-based ART who underwent resistance testing following confirmed virological failure (HIV RNA ≥1000 copies/mL) after six months of enhanced adherence support were included in the analysis.",The paper states that 28 individuals on DTG-based ART who underwent resistance testing were included in the analysis.
41023944,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024. Of 28 individual tested, 13 (46.4%) exhibited DTG resistance (12/13 with intermediate or high-level resistance); 92.3% harboured HIV-1 subtype C. Frequently observed mutations included G118R (43.8%), E138K (43.8%), L74M (31.3%), and R263K (25.0%), often in combination. All DTG-resistant individuals who underwent testing for other drug classes (n = 9) also showed co-resistance to NRTIs and/or NNRTIs. Notably, high-level resistance emerged also in 6 failing participants having shifted to DTG while virologically suppressed.","The study included individuals on DTG-based ART, 13 (46.4%) exhibited DTG resistance, suggesting they had previously received ARV drugs."
41023944,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI","All DTG-resistant individuals who underwent testing for other drug classes (n = 9) also showed co-resistance to NRTIs and/or NNRTIs. Notably, high-level resistance emerged also in 6 failing participants having shifted to DTG while virologically suppressed. Among the 16 individuals tested for resistance to other antiretroviral classes, all were susceptible to protease inhibitors (PI). However, co-resistance was identified in all nine DTG-resistant individuals who underwent testing for other drug classes: eight were resistant to both nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) and non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs), and one to NRTIs only. The most frequent mutations were M184V and K65R, while no major PI resistance mutations were detected.","The paper mentions the testing of resistance to NRTIs, NNRTIs, and that all were susceptible to PI."
41023944,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,Not specified,"All DTG-resistant individuals who underwent testing for other drug classes (n = 9) also showed co-resistance to NRTIs and/or NNRTIs. Notably, high-level resistance emerged also in 6 failing participants having shifted to DTG while virologically suppressed. Among the 16 individuals tested for resistance to other antiretroviral classes, all were susceptible to protease inhibitors (PI). However, co-resistance was identified in all nine DTG-resistant individuals who underwent testing for other drug classes: eight were resistant to both nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) and non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTIs), and one to NRTIs only. The most frequent mutations were M184V and K65R, while no major PI resistance mutations were detected.",The specific drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing are not detailed in the provided content.
41023944,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,The datasets used and/or analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.,"The paper states that the datasets used and/or analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request, which does not imply public availability."
41029850,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the ""Chantal Biya"" International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon. Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing and analysed using Stanford HIVdatabase.v.9.4.","The study involved sequencing HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes from patient samples with confirmed virological failure, indicating that sequences were obtained from patient samples."
41029850,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the ""Chantal Biya"" International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon. Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing and analysed using Stanford HIVdatabase.v.9.4.","The study focused on genotypic resistance testing from patient samples and did not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility assays or data, indicating that in vitro susceptiblity was not performed or reported."
41029850,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,"PQ893197, PQ893192, OQ985810, OR259747, PQ893191","sequences have been submitted to Genebank with available accession numbers provided (PQ893197, PQ893192, OQ985810, OR259747 and PQ893191) in the current version of the manuscript","The paper explicitly lists the GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, confirming their availability."
41029850,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Protease and reverse transcriptase,Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing.,The paper mentions sequencing of the HIV-1 protease and reverse transcriptase genes from the patient samples.
41029850,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Cameroon,"We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the ""Chantal Biya"" International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon.","The study was conducted at the Chantal Biya International Reference Centre in Yaoundé, Cameroon, and the samples were obtained from this location."
41029850,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2022-2023,"We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the ""Chantal Biya"" International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon. Individuals received for GRT were consecutively enrolled at CIRCB, between January 2022 to April 2023.",The study period during which the samples were obtained is clearly stated as October 2022 through April 2023.
41029850,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing.,The paper specifies that Sanger sequencing was employed for sequencing the HIV-1 genes in the study.
41029850,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing.,"The study does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, indicating that this was not done."
41029850,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"GRT was performed in the pol gene (protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT)) on the stored samples with plasma viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml, using the previously validated inhouse protocol as described by Fokam et al. [21].","The paper mentions sequencing of samples with plasma viral load  ≥ 1000 copies/ml, indicating that plasma samples were sequenced."
41029850,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the ""Chantal Biya"" International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon. Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing and analysed using Stanford HIVdatabase.v.9.4.",The study specifically involved sequencing samples from individuals with confirmed virological failure on their treatment regimen.
41029850,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the ""Chantal Biya"" International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon.","The study is described as a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study, not a clinical trial, indicating that the patients were not part of a clinical trial."
41029850,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,203,"Of 2109 individuals assessed, 203 met inclusion criteria, median age 37 [16-47] years and 118/203 (58.13%) females.",The paper states that 203 individuals met the inclusion criteria for the study and had samples obtained for sequencing.
41029850,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the ""Chantal Biya"" International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon. Participants were on documented ART for at least 12 months and experiencing virological failure (viral load ≥ 1000 copies/ml).","The study involved participants who were on documented ART for at least 12 months, indicating they had previously received ARV drugs."
41029850,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI/r","The median [IQR] duration on ART was 10 [6.9-13.3] years, with majority (160, 78.70%) on second-line, 34 (16.80%) on first-line and 9 (4.50%) on third-line. Detailed regimens included: ABC + 3TC + EFV/NVP (0.98%); AZT + 3TC + EFV/NVP (1.47) TDF + 3TC + EFV/NVP (6.89%); TDF + 3TC + DTG (7.39%%); AZT + 3TC + DTG (0.49%); ABC + 3TC + DTG (3.94%); ABC + 3TC + ATV/r (17.73%); ABC + 3TC + LPV/r (6.40%); AZT + 3TC + ATV/r (15.76%), AZT + 3TC + LVP/r (5.42%); TDF + 3TC + ATV/r (29.56%); TDF + 3TC + LPV/r (3.94%).","The paper lists various treatment regimens utilized by the participants, detailing the classes of ARV drugs received, including NRTIs, NNRTIs, and PIs."
41029850,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"ABC, 3TC, EFV, NVP, AZT, TDF, DTG, ATV, LPV","Detailed regimens included: ABC + 3TC + EFV/NVP (0.98%); AZT + 3TC + EFV/NVP (1.47) TDF + 3TC + EFV/NVP (6.89%); TDF + 3TC + DTG (7.39%%); AZT + 3TC + DTG (0.49%); ABC + 3TC + DTG (3.94%); ABC + 3TC + ATV/r (17.73%); ABC + 3TC + LPV/r (6.40%); AZT + 3TC + ATV/r (15.76%), AZT + 3TC + LVP/r (5.42%); TDF + 3TC + ATV/r (29.56%); TDF + 3TC + LPV/r (3.94%).",The paper specifies the individual drugs that were part of the treatment regimens received by the participants before sequencing.
41029850,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"However, sequences have been submitted to Genebank with available accession numbers provided (PQ893197, PQ893192, OQ985810, OR259747 and PQ893191) in the current version of the manuscript while the rest will be completed when made available by Genbank (submission ID: 2961867) (lines 451 to 455)","The paper mentions that sequences were submitted to GenBank and the accession numbers are provided, indicating that the sequences were made publicly available."
41056006,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,Emergence of LEN resistance was an exploratory endpoint. HIV-1 capsid genotypic and phenotypic data were assessed at study entry using the GenoSure Gag-Pro and PhenoSense Gag-Pro assays (Monogram Biosciences).,"The paper mentions the assessment of HIV-1 genotypic and phenotypic data, but it does not indicate that actual sequences from patient samples were reported."
41056006,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Emergence of LEN resistance was an exploratory endpoint. HIV-1 capsid genotypic and phenotypic data were assessed at study entry using the GenoSure Gag-Pro and PhenoSense Gag-Pro assays (Monogram Biosciences). Resistance testing was performed for any participants who met one of the following virologic failure criteria: suboptimal virological response, defined as two consecutive visits with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL and a decline of <1log10 from Day 1 to Week 10; virological rebound, defined as two consecutive visits with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL after reaching <50 copies/mL, or two consecutive visits with an increase from nadir of >1log10; or participants with HIV-1 RNA ≥50 copies/mL at last study visit.",The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility assays or results related to the susceptibility of HIV to antiviral drugs.
41056006,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,"Data availability statement. Gilead Sciences shares anonymized individual participant data upon request or as required by law or regulation with qualified external researchers based on submitted curriculum vitae and reflecting non-conflict of interest. The request proposal must also include a statistician. Approval of such requests is at Gilead Science's discretion and is dependent on the nature of the request, the merit of the research proposed, the availability of the data, and the intended use of the data. Data requests should be sent to datarequest@gilead.com.","The paper did not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced isolates, and the data are available upon request."
41056006,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Not reported,Emergence of LEN resistance was an exploratory endpoint. HIV-1 capsid genotypic and phenotypic data were assessed at study entry using the GenoSure Gag-Pro and PhenoSense Gag-Pro assays (Monogram Biosciences).,The paper does not specify which genes were sequenced.
41056006,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,"USA, Dominican Republic","Eligible participants were treatment naïve, ≥18 years old, and had screening plasma HIV-1 RNA of ≥200 copies/mL and CD4 cell counts of ≥200 cells/μL without active hepatitis B or C infection. All participants provided written informed consent. Study Design and Population. CALIBRATE was a Phase 2, randomized, open-label, active-controlled, multicenter study. Treatment-naive PwH were enrolled across 41 sites in the USA and Dominican Republic (study design presented in Fig. 1).",The samples were obtained from treatment-naive participants enrolled across 41 sites in the USA and Dominican Republic.
41056006,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2019-2020,"Between 22 November 2019 and 27 August 2020, 183 participants in CALIBRATE were randomly assigned to one of four TGs.",The paper states that participants were enrolled between November 2019 and August 2020.
