41057785,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI","Most PLHIV were initiated on NNRTIs as the anchor drug (126, 54.8%), whereas the most initiated backbone was AZT(Zidovudine)-based (93, 40.8%). Most DRM testing followed second-line failure (189, 80.4%), mainly on NRTI + PI (78, 40.6%).","The paper mentions the patients were initiated on NRTI,NNRTI,PI or INSTI based regimen."
41057785,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"AZT, D4T, TDF, ABC, DTG","Most PLHIV were initiated on NNRTIs as the anchor drug (126, 54.8%), whereas the most initiated backbone was AZT(Zidovudine)-based (93, 40.8%). Most DRM testing followed second-line failure (189, 80.4%), mainly on NRTI + PI (78, 40.6%).","The paper mentions the patients were initiated on NRTI,NNRTI,PI or INSTI based regimen."
41057785,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,Methods This cohort study retrospectively reviewed medical records of 235 People living with HIV (PLHIV) who underwent HIV drug resistance testing at the Mildmay-Uganda Hospital from 2018 to 2023.,The paper does not mention any publicly available sequences.
41088231,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"In the previous drug resistance surveillance among 5602 HIV-1-infected antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naive individuals in Guangdong Province from Jan. 2023 to Dec. 2023, we identified one partial pol gene sequence in a plasma specimen collected from an HIV-1-infected patient, 230794, as a suspected recombinant of subtype F2 and CRF02_AG, two subtypes of HIV that are very rare in China and even worldwide. Viral RNA was extracted from the 2023 plasma sample using a magnetic bead-based method. RNA was reverse transcribed to cDNA using the SuperScript III System. Proviral DNA was extracted from the whole-blood sample collected in 2025 with the Viral DNA Extraction Kit (Supbio, China). Two overlapping halves of the HIV-1 genome were subsequently amplified by nested polymerase chain reaction (PCR) by utilizing LA Taq (TaKaRa, China) with a near endpoint dilution method as described previously.","The paper discusses the identification and sequencing of HIV-1 from patient samples, specifically mentioning the collection of plasma and whole-blood samples from patient 230794 in Guangdong Province, China."
41088231,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods The near full-length genome (NFLG) of HIV-1 was amplified with two overlapping segments by the near endpoint dilution method. The positive products were sequenced directly to obtain genomic sequences. The recombinant pattern and breakpoints of the NFLG were determined using the Simplot software and confirmed by maximum likelihood (ML) trees for segments using the IQtree tool. The maximum clade credibility (MCC) tree was reconstructed to trace the origin by using BEAST software. Genotypic drug resistance data were interpreted by the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database. The co-receptor tropism was predicted using the geno2pheno coreceptor 2.5 tool, the WebPSSM tool and the HIVcoPRED tool.",The paper focuses on genomic sequencing and analysis of HIV-1 from patient samples and does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data.
41088231,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,PV187006,The NFLG sequence of strain 230794 was 8260 nucleotides (nt) in length and submitted to GenBank (accession number PV187006).,The paper explicitly states that the NFLG sequence of strain 230794 was submitted to GenBank with the accession number PV187006.
41088231,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Near full-length genome (NFLG), partial pol gene, vif, env",The near full-length genome (NFLG) of HIV-1 was amplified with two overlapping segments by the near endpoint dilution method. The positive products were sequenced directly to obtain genomic sequences. The recombinant pattern and breakpoints of the NFLG were determined using the Simplot software and confirmed by maximum likelihood (ML) trees for segments using the IQtree tool. The V3-loop sequence of the NFLG was obtained using the GeneCutter tool.,The paper mentions sequencing the near full-length genome (NFLG) which includes the vif and env genes and the partial pol gene.
41088231,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"Specimen 230794 was collected in Oct. 2023 from a 47-year-old man who claimed to have contracted HIV-1 through heterosexual contact in Guangzhou City, Guangdong Province, China. In the previous drug resistance surveillance among 5602 HIV-1-infected antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naive individuals in Guangdong Province from Jan. 2023 to Dec. 2023, we identified one partial pol gene sequence in a plasma specimen collected from an HIV-1-infected patient, 230794, as a suspected recombinant of subtype F2 and CRF02_AG, two subtypes of HIV that are very rare in China and even worldwide. To date, another 9 NFLGs associated with the recombination of CRF02_AG and subtype F2 [8-10] have been submitted to GenBank according to the Los Alamos HIV sequence database, all of which differ from the breakpoints of strain 230794 after comparison (as shown in Fig. 3).","The paper specifies that the samples were obtained from Guangdong Province, China."
41088231,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,"2023, 2025","Specimen 230794 was collected in Oct. 2023 from a 47-year-old man who claimed to have contracted HIV-1 through heterosexual contact in Guangzhou City, Guangdong Province, China. From the 2023 plasma sample, we successfully amplified and sequenced four SGA sequences for the 5′ half-genome and the 3′ half-genome respectively. From the 2025 whole blood sample, we successfully amplified and sequenced three SGA sequences from the 5′ half-genome and the 3′ half-genome respectively.","The paper mentions the plasma specimen was collected in 2023, and the whole blood specimen was collected in 2025."
41088231,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing method,"Positive products were directly purified and sequenced by Sangon Biotech (Shanghai) Co., Ltd with Sanger sequencing method.",The paper states that positive products were purified and sequenced using the Sanger sequencing method.
41088231,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Positive products were directly purified and sequenced by Sangon Biotech (Shanghai) Co., Ltd with Sanger sequencing method. Two overlapping halves of the HIV-1 genome were subsequently amplified by nested polymerase chain reaction (PCR) by utilizing LA Taq (TaKaRa, China) with a near endpoint dilution method as described previously.","The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, which commonly used direct sequencing."
41088231,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,"Plasma, whole blood","Specimen 230794 was collected in Oct. 2023 from a 47-year-old man who claimed to have contracted HIV-1 through heterosexual contact in Guangzhou City, Guangdong Province, China. Viral RNA was extracted from the 2023 plasma sample using a magnetic bead-based method. RNA was reverse transcribed to cDNA using the SuperScript III System. Proviral DNA was extracted from the whole-blood sample collected in 2025 with the Viral DNA Extraction Kit (Supbio, China). Two overlapping halves of the HIV-1 genome were subsequently amplified by nested polymerase chain reaction (PCR) by utilizing LA Taq (TaKaRa, China) with a near endpoint dilution method as described previously.",The paper specifies that viral RNA was extracted from a plasma sample and proviral DNA was extracted from a whole-blood sample.
41088231,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"In the previous drug resistance surveillance among 5602 HIV-1-infected antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naive individuals in Guangdong Province from Jan. 2023 to Dec. 2023, we identified one partial pol gene sequence in a plasma specimen collected from an HIV-1-infected patient, 230794, as a suspected recombinant of subtype F2 and CRF02_AG, two subtypes of HIV that are very rare in China and even worldwide. In Jan. 2024, three months after initiation of BIC + FTC + TAF treatment, the plasma HIV-1 RNA viral load of patient 230794 was still at 369 IU/mL which was above the detection threshold of the assay used, warranting further investigation. To enhance virological suppression, the patient was switched to a salvage regime of BIC + FTC + TAF and Lopinavir + RTV + Raltegravir, which led to successful suppression (< 50 IU/mL after 8 months of ART), with near full-sequence of the near full-length genome (NFLG) viral sequences available. The baseline HIV-1 viral load was ~3.95×10^5 IU/mL, and the CD4+ T-cell count was 77 cells/μL. These values indicated that this patient was in the advanced stage of HIV-1 infection, which had not been identified and treated in a timely manner, resulting in severe immune system damage.",The paper indicates the patient was ART-naïve at enrollment and no sequence from individuals with virological failure was reported.
41088231,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"In the previous drug resistance surveillance among 5602 HIV-1-infected antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naive individuals in Guangdong Province from Jan. 2023 to Dec. 2023, we identified one partial pol gene sequence in a plasma specimen collected from an HIV-1-infected patient, 230794, as a suspected recombinant of subtype F2 and CRF02_AG, two subtypes of HIV that are very rare in China and even worldwide. In Jan. 2024, three months after initiation of BIC + FTC + TAF treatment, the plasma HIV-1 RNA viral load of patient 230794 was still at 369 IU/mL which was above the detection threshold of the assay used, warranting further investigation. To enhance virological suppression, the patient was switched to a salvage regime of BIC + FTC + TAF and Lopinavir + RTV + Raltegravir, which led to successful suppression (< 50 IU/mL after 8 months of ART), with near full-sequence of the near full-length genome (NFLG) viral sequences available. The baseline HIV-1 viral load was ~3.95×10^5 IU/mL, and the CD4+ T-cell count was 77 cells/μL. These values indicated that this patient was in the advanced stage of HIV-1 infection, which had not been identified and treated in a timely manner, resulting in severe immune system damage.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. It focuses on drug resistance surveillance and genetic analysis of HIV-1 from reported a single case study.
41088231,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,1,"In the previous drug resistance surveillance among 5602 HIV-1-infected antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naive individuals in Guangdong Province from Jan. 2023 to Dec. 2023, we identified one partial pol gene sequence in a plasma specimen collected from an HIV-1-infected patient, 230794, as a suspected recombinant of subtype F2 and CRF02_AG, two subtypes of HIV that are very rare in China and even worldwide. Specimen 230794 was collected in Oct. 2023 from a 47-year-old man who claimed to have contracted HIV-1 through heterosexual contact in Guangzhou City, Guangdong Province, China.",The paper mentions that only one patient 230794 had their HIV-1 sequenced and described the sequencing process and results in detail.
41088231,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"In the previous drug resistance surveillance among 5602 HIV-1-infected antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naive individuals in Guangdong Province from Jan. 2023 to Dec. 2023, we identified one partial pol gene sequence in a plasma specimen collected from an HIV-1-infected patient, 230794, as a suspected recombinant of subtype F2 and CRF02_AG, two subtypes of HIV that are very rare in China and even worldwide. In Jan. 2024, three months after initiation of BIC + FTC + TAF treatment, the plasma HIV-1 RNA viral load of patient 230794 was still at 369 IU/mL which was above the detection threshold of the assay used, warranting further investigation. To enhance virological suppression, the patient was switched to a salvage regime of BIC + FTC + TAF and Lopinavir + RTV + Raltegravir, which led to successful suppression (< 50 IU/mL after 8 months of ART), with near full-sequence of the near full-length genome (NFLG) viral sequences available. The baseline HIV-1 viral load was ~3.95×10^5 IU/mL, and the CD4+ T-cell count was 77 cells/μL. These values indicated that this patient was in the advanced stage of HIV-1 infection, which had not been identified and treated in a timely manner, resulting in severe immune system damage.","The paper explicitly states that the sample was from an ART-naïve individual, at the time of sample collection in 2023, and  the study was previous drug resistance surveillance."
41088231,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"In the previous drug resistance surveillance among 5602 HIV-1-infected antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naive individuals in Guangdong Province from Jan. 2023 to Dec. 2023, we identified one partial pol gene sequence in a plasma specimen collected from an HIV-1-infected patient, 230794, as a suspected recombinant of subtype F2 and CRF02_AG, two subtypes of HIV that are very rare in China and even worldwide. In Jan. 2024, three months after initiation of BIC + FTC + TAF treatment, the plasma HIV-1 RNA viral load of patient 230794 was still at 369 IU/mL which was above the detection threshold of the assay used, warranting further investigation. To enhance virological suppression, the patient was switched to a salvage regime of BIC + FTC + TAF and Lopinavir + RTV + Raltegravir, which led to successful suppression (< 50 IU/mL after 8 months of ART), with near full-sequence of the near full-length genome (NFLG) viral sequences available. The baseline HIV-1 viral load was ~3.95×10^5 IU/mL, and the CD4+ T-cell count was 77 cells/μL. These values indicated that this patient was in the advanced stage of HIV-1 infection, which had not been identified and treated in a timely manner, resulting in severe immune system damage.","The paper states that the patient was antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve at the time of initial sample collection in 2023, and hadn't received any drugs prior to sample sequencing."
41088231,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"In the previous drug resistance surveillance among 5602 HIV-1-infected antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naive individuals in Guangdong Province from Jan. 2023 to Dec. 2023, we identified one partial pol gene sequence in a plasma specimen collected from an HIV-1-infected patient, 230794, as a suspected recombinant of subtype F2 and CRF02_AG, two subtypes of HIV that are very rare in China and even worldwide. In Jan. 2024, three months after initiation of BIC + FTC + TAF treatment, the plasma HIV-1 RNA viral load of patient 230794 was still at 369 IU/mL which was above the detection threshold of the assay used, warranting further investigation. To enhance virological suppression, the patient was switched to a salvage regime of BIC + FTC + TAF and Lopinavir + RTV + Raltegravir, which led to successful suppression (< 50 IU/mL after 8 months of ART), with near full-sequence of the near full-length genome (NFLG) viral sequences available. The baseline HIV-1 viral load was ~3.95×10^5 IU/mL, and the CD4+ T-cell count was 77 cells/μL. These values indicated that this patient was in the advanced stage of HIV-1 infection, which had not been identified and treated in a timely manner, resulting in severe immune system damage.","The paper states that the patient was antiretroviral therapy (ART)-naïve at the time of initial sample collection in 2023, and hadn't received any drugs prior to sample sequencing."
41088231,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,The NFLG sequence of strain 230794 was 8260 nucleotides (nt) in length and submitted to GenBank (accession number PV187006).,The paper specifies that the NFLG sequence of strain 230794 was submitted to GenBank with the accession number PV187006.
41091504,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The large number of drug-resistant sequences from patient samples and viral evolution experiments have resulted in a plethora of experimental data from us and others, including matched enzyme inhibition potencies and cocryystal structures.","The paper does not mention any specific sequences obtained from patient samples, focusing instead on computational modeling and simulations."
41091504,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,Our optimized strategy involves rerefining cocryystal structures to ensure accurate inhibitor geometries and combining parallel molecular dynamics simulations with feature selection to develop a robust machine learning model to predict loss of potency and resistance.,"The paper does not include in vitro drug susceptibility data, as it primarily discusses modeling and predictions."
