41093949,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"The wild-type infectious cloning plasmid pGX002 of CRF01_AE is a full-length genomic infectious clone of the HIV-1 CRF01_AE strain, which was constructed by the local laboratory of the Academy of Military Medical Sciences. Both plasmids are suitable for packaging infectious virus particles and contain an ampicillin resistance gene.",The paper does not mention any patient samples or sequences derived from patients. It focuses on laboratory constructs and experiments with infectious clones.
41093949,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"The same RTI-induced DRMs were introduced into infectious clones of subtypes B and CRF01_AE, and phenotypic resistance tests were performed to compare the differences in susceptibilities to various RTIs. The experimental results revealed that mutations such as M41L and V106M were more likely to occur in the CRF01_AE subtype. However, phenotypic analysis of reconstructed clones containing these resistance mutations revealed that CRF01_AE exhibited significantly lower sensitivity to RTIs than did subtype B. For azvudine (FNC), a novel nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI), the resistance level of CRF01_AE was significantly lower than that of subtype B.","The paper includes experimental results that detail the in vitro susceptibility of HIV-1 strains to various reverse-transcriptase inhibitors, indicating that drug susceptibility data is reported."
41093949,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,PQ735970-PQ735985,The NCBI repository accession numbers for the AE/B recombinants are: PQ735970-PQ735985.,The GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates (in the context of laboratory constructs) are explicitly stated in the results section.
41093949,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Pol region,"The HIV-1 CRF01_AE and subtype B Pol region sequences were downloaded from the HIV Sequence Database (https://www.hiv.lanl.gov/) according to the following criteria: (1) from database creation to December 31,2023; (2) country labeled as CN (China) or US (United States); (3) complete information (geographic location, year the sequence was sampled, earliest sequence taken from same subject) available; and (4) contained the Pol region.",The Pol region is mentioned as the sequenced region used for constructing the recombinant viruses.
41093949,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,"China, United States","The HIV-1 CRF01_AE and subtype B Pol region sequences were downloaded from the HIV Sequence Database (https://www.hiv.lanl.gov/) according to the following criteria: (1) from database creation to December 31,2023; (2) country labeled as CN (China) or US (United States); (3) complete information (geographic location, year the sequence was sampled, earliest sequence taken from same subject) available; and (4) contained the Pol region.",The paper specifies that the sequences were obtained from the USA and China.
41093949,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,1978 to 2023,"A total of 8654 subtype B sequences (76.36% from the USA) and 10,551 CRF01_AE sequences (0.07% from the USA) were obtained after sorting and were sampled from 1978 to 2023. In subtype B, the top five prevalent mutation sites for NTRI were as follows: 184 (17.40%), 215 (15.24%), 41 (10.77%), 67 (9.33%) and 219 (7.15%) for NRTI DRMs.",The paper provides the year range from 1978 to 2023.
41093949,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"Positive clones were picked and amplified in LB broth containing ampicillin, followed by Sanger sequencing (by SinoGenoMax).",The paper mentions that positive clones were confirmed by Sanger sequencing.
41093949,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,Yes,"The virus was gradient diluted in transparent 96-well plates. The virus was diluted 10-fold as the highest concentration, and then a 3-fold gradient was used to dilute the virus to well 7, with the last well used as a blank control. The diluted virus was added to 96-well containing with TZM-bl cells, with four replicate wells for each virus dilution. After incubation for 48h in a thermostatically controlled cell incubator, the relative luminescence units (RLU) of the cells were determined on an EnSpire plate reader by adding a Bright-Glo fluorescence mixture (Promega, Cat. No. E2650). With 2.5 times the RLU of the blank control wells used as the cut-off, a well was considered positive when the RLU of the test well was greater than the cut-off. Median tissue culture infective dose (TCID50) values were calculated for each virus strain according to Eqs. (1) and (2).","The paper mentions that eight million HEK293T cells were plated in 10cm cell culture dishes, 10ml of medium was added, and the cells were cultured overnight to 70 - 90% confluence. The  transfected cell culture dishes were transferred to a BSL-3 laboratory and cultured in a cell constant temperature incubator at 37°C and 5%CO2 for 48h. After 48h of incubation in a constant-temperature incubator, CellTiter-Glo* (Promega, Cat. No. G7573) was added to assess cell viability to confirm the cytotoxicity of each drug. Each drug was diluted to the working concentration. The gradient diluted drug and 200TCID50 of virus were added to a 96-well plate containing 9900 TZM-bl cells per well, with two duplicate wells for each drug and virus, and the experiment was repeated three times. After incubation in a thermostatic incubator for 48h, the BrightGlo fluorescence mixture was added, the relative chemiluminescence of the cells was determined on an EnSpire multiplate reader, and the half maximal inhibitory concentration (IC50) value was calculated with GraphPad Prism v9.0.0. Statistical analysis was performed with IBM SPSS Statistics 25 (SPSS v25.0) to calculate the mean and standard deviation of the IC50 from triplicate experiments for each drug and strain, and the Shapiro-Wilk test was used to confirm that the data followed a normal distribution. The multiplicity of the IC50 of the resistant strain relative to the wild-type strain was calculated as the fold change (FC). The FC classifications were as follows: susceptible, ≤ 4 FC; resistant, >4 to 10 FC; and high-level resistance, >10FC. In GraphPad Prism v9.0.0, the effect of the same resistance mutation on antiretroviral activity between genetic backgrounds of different subtypes was assessed by a parametric unpaired Student’s t-test with Welch’s correction, which does not assume equal standard deviations."
41093949,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,"Plasmid clones, Recombinant viruses","The same RTI-induced DRMs were introduced into infectious clones of subtypes B and CRF01_AE, and phenotypic resistance tests were performed to compare the differences in susceptibilities to various RTIs. The experimental results revealed that mutations such as M41L and V106M were more likely to occur in the CRF01_AE subtype. However, phenotypic analysis of reconstructed clones containing these resistance mutations revealed that CRF01_AE exhibited significantly lower sensitivity to RTIs than did subtype B. For azvudine (FNC), a novel nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI), the resistance level of CRF01_AE was significantly lower than that of subtype B.",The types of samples sequnced were not from patient were it from lab constructed recombinant viruses.
41093949,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"The same RTI-induced DRMs were introduced into infectious clones of subtypes B and CRF01_AE, and phenotypic resistance tests were performed to compare the differences in susceptibilities to various RTIs. The experimental results revealed that mutations such as M41L and V106M were more likely to occur in the CRF01_AE subtype. However, phenotypic analysis of reconstructed clones containing these resistance mutations revealed that CRF01_AE exhibited significantly lower sensitivity to RTIs than did subtype B. For azvudine (FNC), a novel nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI), the resistance level of CRF01_AE was significantly lower than that of subtype B.",The paper does not mention any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure. It focuses on laboratory constructs and experiments.
41093949,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The same RTI-induced DRMs were introduced into infectious clones of subtypes B and CRF01_AE, and phenotypic resistance tests were performed to compare the differences in susceptibilities to various RTIs. The experimental results revealed that mutations such as M41L and V106M were more likely to occur in the CRF01_AE subtype. However, phenotypic analysis of reconstructed clones containing these resistance mutations revealed that CRF01_AE exhibited significantly lower sensitivity to RTIs than did subtype B. For azvudine (FNC), a novel nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI), the resistance level of CRF01_AE was significantly lower than that of subtype B.",The paper does not mention any clinical trials or patient involvement. It focuses on laboratory constructs and experiments.
41093949,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,0,"The same RTI-induced DRMs were introduced into infectious clones of subtypes B and CRF01_AE, and phenotypic resistance tests were performed to compare the differences in susceptibilities to various RTIs. The experimental results revealed that mutations such as M41L and V106M were more likely to occur in the CRF01_AE subtype. However, phenotypic analysis of reconstructed clones containing these resistance mutations revealed that CRF01_AE exhibited significantly lower sensitivity to RTIs than did subtype B. For azvudine (FNC), a novel nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI), the resistance level of CRF01_AE was significantly lower than that of subtype B.",The paper does not mention any individuals from whom samples were obtained for sequencing. It focuses on laboratory constructs and experiments.
41093949,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"The same RTI-induced DRMs were introduced into infectious clones of subtypes B and CRF01_AE, and phenotypic resistance tests were performed to compare the differences in susceptibilities to various RTIs. The experimental results revealed that mutations such as M41L and V106M were more likely to occur in the CRF01_AE subtype. However, phenotypic analysis of reconstructed clones containing these resistance mutations revealed that CRF01_AE exhibited significantly lower sensitivity to RTIs than did subtype B. For azvudine (FNC), a novel nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI), the resistance level of CRF01_AE was significantly lower than that of subtype B.",The paper does not mention any sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs. It focuses on laboratory constructs and experiments.
41093949,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs","As the recommended drugs for first-line regimens in HIV-1 infections, reverse-transcriptase inhibitors (RTIs) are widely used around the world. To escape from the selective pressure imposed by RTIs, HIV-1 constantly mutates to adapt to its environment. However, it is unclear how resistance mutations emerging in response to RTIs affect drug susceptibility in strains of different subtypes.","The paper mentions that it studied the effects of reverse-transcriptase inhibitors (RTIs)  on HIV-1 strains, but the samples were not from individuals."
41093949,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI: azvudine, zidovudine, lamivudine, tenofovir DF, abacavir, emtricitabine; NNRTI: nevirapine, etravirine, efavirenz","Reverse transcriptase inhibitors The following drugs were purchased from Selleck Company in powder form and had purities greater than 99.8%: zidovudine (AZT, Cat. No. S2579), lamivudine (3TC, Cat. No. S1706), tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF, Cat. No. S1400), abacavir (ABC, Cat. No. S3165), emtricitabine (FTC, Cat. No. S1704), nevirapine (NVP, Cat. No. S1742), etravirine (ETR, Cat. No. S3080) and efavirenz (EFV, Cat. No. S4685). FNC was kindly provided by Genuine Biotech Limited. All drugs were dissolved in dimethyl sulfoxide and diluted to 10mM in phosphate buffered saline (PBS) (Gibco, Cat. No. 10010002).","The paper lists several NRTIs and NNRTIs, indicating that these drugs were relevant to the study."
41093949,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"The sequences generated during the current study are available in the NCBI repository, PQ735970-PQ735985.",The paper explicitly states that the sequences are publicly available through the NCBI repository.
41112839,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"""A total of 237 samples from different patients were analyzed, and 131 (55.3%) were successfully amplified and sequenced.""","The paper states that a total of 237 samples from different patients were analyzed, and 131 were successfully sequenced, indicating that HIV sequences from patient samples were reported."
41112839,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The analysis of resistance mutations was carried out using the Calibrated Population Resistance v8.1 program, the proportion of DRM was evaluated globally, and after grouping sites by geographical areas (Dakar, North, Midwest, South-West, and South-East), as shown in Table 1.","The paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data. It focuses on sequencing and analyzing resistance mutations in patient samples, without any in vitro susceptibility assays."
41112839,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,PP280627 to PP280757,The RT-generated sequences were deposited in the National Institutes of Health GenBank database under accession numbers PP280627 to PP280757.,"The paper provides the GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, which range from PP280627 to PP280757."
41112839,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Partial reverse transcriptase (RT) gene,Amplification and sequencing were performed on the partial reverse transcriptase (RT) gene using the Agence Nationale de Recherches sur le Sida et les hepatitis virales (ANRS: French National Agency for Research on acquired immunodeficiency syndrome [AIDS] and Viral Hepatitis)/AC11 method at the HIV National Reference Laboratory.,The paper specifies that the sequencing was performed on the partial reverse transcriptase (RT) gene.
41112839,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Senegal,"Objectives: This study aimed to document the prevalence of pretreatment drug resistance in antiretroviral therapy-naive patients in Senegal, describe mutation profiles, and evaluate their potential impact on future therapy. Methods: A pretreatment drug resistance survey was carried out in 35 sites throughout the country between 2017 and 2018 using dried blood spots and whole blood samples.","The paper was conducted as a national survey in Senegal, and all samples were obtained from various sites within the country."
41112839,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2017 to 2018,PDR surveillance was carried out between 2017 and 2018 according to WHO recommendations.,The paper states that the pretreatment drug resistance survey was carried out between 2017 and 2018.
41112839,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Big Dye Terminator v3.1 and ABI 3100 Avant sequencer,"""Purified and quantified polymerase chain reaction products were sequenced by using Big Dye Terminator v3.1 (Life Technologies, Courtaboeuf, France), and electrophoresis was performed on the ABI 3100 Avant sequencer (Applied Biosystems, France) as previously described.""",The sequencing was performed using the Big Dye Terminator v3.1 method and electrophoresis on the ABI 3100 Avant sequencer.
41112839,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"HIV-1 viral ribonucleic acid (RNA) was extracted on the Nuclisens easyMAG® platform (Biomerieux, France) according to the manufacturer's recommendations. The resulting RNA was used to synthesize complementary deoxyribonucleic acid (cDNA), which was amplified using the ANRS AC11 genotyping technique targeting the first 240 amino acids of RT. Primer pairs used for polymerase chain reaction were MJ3/MJ4 and A35/N35.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing. The method described involves direct amplification and sequencing of the viral RNA.
41112839,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Dried blood spots (DBS) and plasma samples,"Most of the samples received were DBS (n = 121) originating primarily from the Southern part of the country with 97 (40.9%) and 34 (22.8%) samples from the Southeast and Southwest areas, respectively. Only 17 samples were received as whole blood.",The paper states that the samples sequenced included dried blood spots (DBS) and plasma samples.
41112839,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"The inclusion criteria for patients in the sites were to be HIV-1-positive men or women, aged at least 18, ART-naive (defined as never having received any antiretroviral therapy, including prevention of mother-to-child transmission [PMTCT] regimens), and newly diagnosed based on the patient and the healthcare provider declarations.","The paper included only antiretroviral therapy-naive patients, specifically targeting those who had never received any antiretroviral therapy, including PMTCT regimens."
41112839,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Objectives: This study aimed to document the prevalence of pretreatment drug resistance in antiretroviral therapy-naive patients in Senegal, describe mutation profiles, and evaluate their potential impact on future therapy. The inclusion criteria for patients in the sites were to be HIV-1-positive men or women, aged at least 18, ART-naive (defined as never having received any antiretroviral therapy, including prevention of mother-to-child transmission [PMTCT] regimens), and newly diagnosed based on the patient and the healthcare provider declarations.",The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial. The study was conducted as a national surveillance of drug resistance.
41112839,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,237,"A total of 237 samples from different patients were analyzed, and 131 (55.3%) were successfully amplified and sequenced.",The paper states that a total of 237 samples from different patients were analyzed for sequencing.
41112839,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,"The inclusion criterion for patients in the sites were to be HIV-1-positive men or women, aged at least 18, ART-naive (defined as never having received any antiretroviral therapy, including prevention of mother-to-child transmission [PMTCT] regimens), and newly diagnosed based on the patient and the healthcare provider declarations,  the sample were dried blood spots and venous blood. The study was conducted according to WHO recommendations to fill the knowledge gap.","The paper was focused on antiretroviral therapy-naive patients, specifically those who had never received any antiretroviral therapy."
41112839,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"The inclusion criteria for patients in the sites were to be HIV-1-positive men or women, aged at least 18, ART-naive (defined as never having received any antiretroviral therapy, including prevention of mother-to-child transmission [PMTCT] regimens), and newly diagnosed based on the patient and the healthcare provider declarations.","All individuals in the study were antiretroviral therapy-naive, meaning they had not received any drug classes before sample sequencing."
41112839,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"The inclusion criteria for patients in the sites were to be HIV-1-positive men or women, aged at least 18, ART-naive (defined as never having received any antiretroviral therapy, including prevention of mother-to-child transmission [PMTCT] regimens), and newly diagnosed based on the patient and the healthcare provider declarations.","All individuals in the study were antiretroviral therapy-naive, meaning they had not received any drugs before sample sequencing."
41112839,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,The RT-generated sequences were deposited in the National Institutes of Health GenBank database under accession numbers PP280627 to PP280757.,"The paper states that the sequences were deposited in the National Institutes of Health GenBank database, indicating they were made publicly available."
41129268,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Among 333 successfully genotyped patient samples, 122 (36.6%) CLHIV were on a DTG-based regimen and 136 (40.8%) on a lopinavir-based regimen.","The study mentions genotyping of patient samples, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from these samples."
41129268,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,Yes,"Drug susceptibility analysis revealed that the majority of CLHIV (78.7%, 262/333) had clinically significant resistance (intermediate-to-high level drug resistance) to at least one ARV, including: DTG (1.8%), LPV (9.6%), DRV (1.8%), NVP (67.0%), EFV (66.1%), 3TC (61.0%), ABC (25.5%), and AZT (19.2%) (Table 2).","The paper discusses drug susceptibility profiles generated using Stanford University's HIVdb algorithm, indicating in vitro susceptibility data."
41129268,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence found.,The paper does not mention any specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
41129268,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"PR, RT, IN","Frequencies of DRMs were calculated overall and stratified by drug class of the mutation, including mutations against NRTIs, NNRTIs, protease inhibitors (PIs), and integrase inhibitors (INSTIs).","The paper mentions mutations being calculated from PR, RT and IN genes."
41129268,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Uganda,Data for this analysis came from previously collected VL samples from children aged ≤ 15 years of age on first-line ART enrolled in any ART-accredited health facility in Uganda whose VL sample satisfied the following criteria,The samples were obtained from children living with HIV in Uganda.
41129268,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2021,Methods: We identified CLHIV aged 0-15 years experiencing virologic failure on first-line antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Uganda between January-November 2021 from the Central Public Health Laboratory database.,The paper specifies that the samples were collected between January and November 2021.
41129268,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"Once a sample fulfilling the inclusion criteria above was identified, it was retrieved and sent to the HIVDR laboratory at Joint Clinical Research Centre to be tested using Sanger sequencing technology if there was sufficient sample volume",The paper states that the samples were tested using Sanger sequencing technology.
41129268,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No evidence found.,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
41129268,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"Data for this analysis came from previously collected VL samples from children aged ≤ 15 years of age on first-line ART enrolled in any ART-accredited health facility in Uganda whose VL sample satisfied the following criteria: (1) sample tested for VL between January and November 2021, (2) sample type was plasma",The paper specifies that the viral load samples tested for HIVDR were plasma samples.
41129268,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Data for this analysis were obtained following the 2020 revision of the Uganda HIV Prevention, Care and Treatment Guidelines that recommended HIVDR testing for all children and adolescents living with HIV failing ART. In 2021, the Uganda Ministry of Health conducted standard-of-care HIVDR testing for CLHIV failing first-line ART to optimize treatment outcomes, through the support of the CDC-funded Faith-based Action for Scaling up Testing and Treatment for the Epidemic Response (FASTER) project. FASTER was implemented between 2019-2021 by Catholic Relief Services, in partnership with the Clinton Health Access Initiative, to support ministries of health in four countries (Uganda, Nigeria, Tanzania and Zambia) to improve testing and treatment of CLHIV",The paper mentioned the inclusion of children living with HIV experiencing virological failure on first-line antiretroviral therapy (ART) in Uganda.
41129268,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"Data for this analysis came from previously collected VL samples from children aged ≤ 15 years of age on first-line ART enrolled in any ART-accredited health facility in Uganda whose VL sample satisfied the following criteria: (1) sample tested for VL between January and November 2021, (2) sample type was plasma (3) VF defined as VL ≥1000 copies/mL for children on EFV or NVP or repeat VL >1000 copies/ml for children on Lopinavir/ritonavir (LPV/r) or DTG. Repeat VL refers to a VL test taken after a child with an initial unsuppressed VL has undergone intensified adherence support for at least 3 months with 3 consecutive good adherence scores. Samples from CLHIV whose prior regimen changes were made for treatment simplification or due to policy changes and not VF were classified as being on first-line ART.",The study focused on standard care HIVDR testing for children living with HIV failing first-line ART and was not conducted as a clinical trial.
41129268,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,333,"Among 333 successfully genotyped patient samples, 122 (36.6%) CLHIV were on a DTG-based regimen and 136 (40.8%) on a lopinavir-based regimen.",The paper states that 333 samples were successfully genotyped from patient samples.
41129268,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Data for this analysis came from previously collected VL samples from children aged ≤ 15 years of age on first-line ART enrolled in any ART-accredited health facility in Uganda whose VL sample satisfied the following criteria: (1) sample tested for VL between January and November 2021, (2) sample type was plasma (3) VF defined as VL ≥1000 copies/mL for children on EFV or NVP or repeat VL >1000 copies/ml for children on Lopinavir/ritonavir (LPV/r) or DTG. Repeat VL refers to a VL test taken after a child with an initial unsuppressed VL has undergone intensified adherence support for at least 3 months with 3 consecutive good adherence scores. Samples from CLHIV whose prior regimen changes were made for treatment simplification or due to policy changes and not VF were classified as being on first-line ART.","The study involved children who were on first-line ART and had experienced virologic failure, indicating prior ARV drug exposure."
41129268,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, INSTIs","Among 333 CLHIV with successful sample amplification, 61 (18.3%) were aged 1-4 years, 79 (23.7%) aged 5-9 years, 89 (26.7%) aged 10-12 years, and 104 (31.2%) aged 13-15 years (Table 1). There were 101 (30.3%) CLHIV taking ART for less than five years, 104 (31.2%) taking ART 5-9 years, and 128 (38.4%) taking ART 10 or more years. At the time of sample collection, 122 (36.6%) CLHIV were taking a DTG-based regimen, 136 (40.8%) were taking an LPV/r-based regimen, and 74 (22.2%) were taking either an EFV- or NVP-based regimen. Among the 122 CLHIV on DTG-based regimens, the median age was 13 years (interquartile range [IQR]=11-14); 54 (44.2%) were on regimens with an ABC/3TC backbone, 65 (53.3%) had a TDF/3TC backbone, and 3(2.5%) had an AZT/3TC backbone.","The paper lists various drug classes that the children had been on, including NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs."
41129268,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Abacavir (ABC), Lamivudine (3TC), Tenofovir (TDF), Zidovudine (AZT), Efavirenz (EFV), Nevirapine (NVP), Lopinavir (LPV), Ritonavir (r), Dolutegravir (DTG)","At the time of sample collection, 122 (36.6%) CLHIV were taking a DTG-based regimen, 136 (40.8%) were taking an LPV/r-based regimen, and 74 (22.2%) were taking either an EFV- or NVP-based regimen. Among the 122 CLHIV on DTG-based regimens, the median age was 13 years (interquartile range [IQR]=11-14); 54 (44.2%) were on regimens with an ABC/3TC backbone, 65 (53.3%) had a TDF/3TC backbone, and 3(2.5%) had an AZT/3TC backbone.",The paper specifies the drugs that were part of the regimens of the children included in the study.
41129268,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence found.,The paper does not mention any public data repository where the sequences have been made available.
41130593,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Participants who acquired HIV-1 with a viral load of   	c200 	copies/mL were evaluated for resistance by genotyping of the HIV-1 capsid, protease, reverse transcriptase and integrase genes at HIV diagnosis.","The paper mentions that HIV-1 sequences were evaluated for resistance by genotyping the HIV-1 capsid, protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes at HIV diagnosis, indicating that sequences from patient samples were reported."
41130593,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,No evidence supports the report of in vitro drug susceptibility data; the phenotyping of clinical isolates was not attempted; no susceptibility data were available.,"The paper mentioned that phenotypic testing of the clinical isolates in the PURPOSE trials was not attempted, indicating that in vitro drug susceptibility data was not reported."
